WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:08.000
&gt;&gt; Such an honor to have Jon Kleinberg visit

00:00:08.001 --> 00:00:12.880
us today for the distinguished lecture at MSR AI.

00:00:12.881 --> 00:00:16.090
Jon is the Tisch University Professor in

00:00:16.091 --> 00:00:17.900
the Departments of Computer Science

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:19.780
and Information Science at Cornell University.

00:00:19.781 --> 00:00:21.500
His research focuses, as you all know,

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.060
on very interesting issues at

00:00:23.061 --> 00:00:24.910
the interface of algorithms, networks,

00:00:24.911 --> 00:00:27.380
and information with an emphasis on

00:00:27.381 --> 00:00:29.230
the social and information networks that

00:00:29.231 --> 00:00:32.660
underpin the web and other media.

00:00:32.661 --> 00:00:36.250
We just came out of a fireside chat that will

00:00:36.251 --> 00:00:39.510
be on hopefully edited and available soon,

00:00:39.511 --> 00:00:40.840
where Jon talked about

00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:43.440
his early days and

00:00:43.441 --> 00:00:46.510
reflections and interest in law and mathematics.

00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:47.920
And wondering if he'd ever

00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:49.890
combine these things together and some issues that

00:00:49.891 --> 00:00:52.250
might come to the fore some day while

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:53.800
staring at an Apple II Computer

00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:55.090
and writing simple programs.

00:00:55.091 --> 00:00:56.650
And the fact that

00:00:56.651 --> 00:00:59.200
he discovered a few years later that this

00:00:59.201 --> 00:01:01.090
is actually a field called computer science

00:01:01.091 --> 00:01:03.755
that brought some of these ideas to life.

00:01:03.756 --> 00:01:05.200
But probably, even back then, he had

00:01:05.201 --> 00:01:06.760
no idea where things were going,

00:01:06.761 --> 00:01:09.950
in terms of the work that though and

00:01:09.951 --> 00:01:11.530
the challenges and opportunities we

00:01:11.531 --> 00:01:13.980
now face as a community.

00:01:13.981 --> 00:01:17.115
Jon's a member of the National Academy of Sciences,

00:01:17.116 --> 00:01:18.830
National Academy of Engineering.

00:01:18.831 --> 00:01:22.040
And the recipient of a fellowship from MacArthur,

00:01:22.041 --> 00:01:23.950
Packard, Simons and the Sloan Foundation.

00:01:23.951 --> 00:01:27.830
And he's won a number of prizes, the Harvey Prize,

00:01:27.831 --> 00:01:30.290
the Nevanlinna Prize,

00:01:30.291 --> 00:01:33.290
Newell Award and the ACM prize in computing.

00:01:33.291 --> 00:01:36.280
Knowing a bunch about your work and following it closely,

00:01:36.281 --> 00:01:38.500
I'm sure all of those prizes were well deserved.

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:41.100
So, Jon. Come on and speak.

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:50.160
&gt;&gt; All right. There's a badge there.

00:01:50.161 --> 00:01:52.260
Great. Well, thanks very much for

00:01:52.261 --> 00:01:55.040
the invitation to come speak here.

00:01:55.041 --> 00:01:58.245
And it's been a really fun day getting to meet with

00:01:58.246 --> 00:01:59.840
so many people whose work

00:01:59.841 --> 00:02:02.650
I really admire and enjoy keeping up with.

00:02:02.651 --> 00:02:06.210
So, I want to talk today about some work

00:02:06.211 --> 00:02:10.065
I've been doing jointly with Sendhil Mullainathan,

00:02:10.066 --> 00:02:11.080
a Behavioral Economist at

00:02:11.081 --> 00:02:12.635
Harvard who have known for a long time.

00:02:12.636 --> 00:02:14.920
And two of my PhD students;

00:02:14.921 --> 00:02:16.880
Manish Raghavan and Maithra Raghu.

00:02:16.881 --> 00:02:19.845
Thinking about issues in

00:02:19.846 --> 00:02:21.670
this emerging space of

00:02:21.671 --> 00:02:24.490
topics that goes under the name Algorithmic Fairness.

00:02:24.491 --> 00:02:26.420
And the talk will really be

00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:29.110
about some definitional questions.

00:02:29.111 --> 00:02:32.775
And really a lot of focus will actually, in this work,

00:02:32.776 --> 00:02:35.290
be about some of the aspects

00:02:35.291 --> 00:02:36.920
where we take

00:02:36.921 --> 00:02:38.690
different competing demands for what it would

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.830
mean for decision rule to be fair and try to

00:02:41.831 --> 00:02:43.470
evaluate how these decisions

00:02:43.471 --> 00:02:45.445
and these definitions interact with each other.

00:02:45.446 --> 00:02:47.695
And I'll try to sort of highlight some things that I

00:02:47.696 --> 00:02:50.380
think we learned that surprised us.

00:02:50.381 --> 00:02:52.170
Once we sort of start to sort

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.190
out some of these definitions conceptual issues.

00:02:55.191 --> 00:02:58.100
So, this actually started

00:02:58.101 --> 00:03:00.150
from some work that Sendhil and I were doing

00:03:00.151 --> 00:03:03.250
on human decision making and

00:03:03.251 --> 00:03:04.790
really look at the intersection of algorithmic

00:03:04.791 --> 00:03:07.190
and human decision making.

00:03:07.191 --> 00:03:09.410
We were motivated by the fact that

00:03:09.411 --> 00:03:13.000
in computer science and in the social sciences,

00:03:13.001 --> 00:03:15.595
their activities that superficially look very different,

00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:18.920
that syntactically have a surprising amount

00:03:18.921 --> 00:03:20.565
in common with each other.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:23.175
So, if I think about computer science,

00:03:23.176 --> 00:03:24.840
a canonical sort of decision that

00:03:24.841 --> 00:03:26.630
we've gotten very good at is the sort

00:03:26.631 --> 00:03:27.720
of decision that you see embodied in

00:03:27.721 --> 00:03:30.045
a ranking function or recommendation function.

00:03:30.046 --> 00:03:31.960
So, this is a screenshot from

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:35.160
a tutorial blog post on Netflix,

00:03:35.161 --> 00:03:36.910
showing how it's making recommendations.

00:03:36.911 --> 00:03:39.190
And essentially from a huge amount

00:03:39.191 --> 00:03:41.370
of data about a user, as all of you know,

00:03:41.371 --> 00:03:44.680
it abstracts some kind of a sparse set of

00:03:44.681 --> 00:03:48.190
features that's somehow a thin representation of this,

00:03:48.191 --> 00:03:50.400
rich, living, breathing individual.

00:03:50.401 --> 00:03:52.070
And it tries to

00:03:52.071 --> 00:03:53.480
predict why am I going to like this movie.

00:03:53.481 --> 00:03:54.300
It's going to assign

00:03:54.301 --> 00:03:55.770
a probability estimate to that based on

00:03:55.771 --> 00:03:56.860
data they have about them and

00:03:56.861 --> 00:03:58.695
data they have about many other people.

00:03:58.696 --> 00:04:02.920
And these kinds of activities in the online world are

00:04:02.921 --> 00:04:04.790
things that we've developed a lot of

00:04:04.791 --> 00:04:07.560
practice at refining and improving.

00:04:07.561 --> 00:04:10.115
But meanwhile, in the offline world

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:13.500
as Sendhil proved to me,

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:15.030
there are activities that human beings are

00:04:15.031 --> 00:04:16.990
doing that look somewhat similar, right?

00:04:16.991 --> 00:04:19.535
So hiring, for example,

00:04:19.536 --> 00:04:22.865
which superficially very different.

00:04:22.866 --> 00:04:24.410
Certainly, each individual

00:04:24.411 --> 00:04:25.730
decision there's more high stakes.

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:28.930
But we take an enviable job applicant who's a complex,

00:04:28.931 --> 00:04:30.290
living, breathing human being.

00:04:30.291 --> 00:04:32.825
They submit something like a resume,

00:04:32.826 --> 00:04:35.595
CV, maybe a school transcript.

00:04:35.596 --> 00:04:37.980
And some hiring committee looks at

00:04:37.981 --> 00:04:40.350
this and decide will this person to be a good employee.

00:04:40.351 --> 00:04:41.590
And in fact, part of

00:04:41.591 --> 00:04:43.005
that decision is to make a prediction;

00:04:43.006 --> 00:04:45.570
will they do well in the environment at our company?

00:04:45.571 --> 00:04:48.070
Same thing happens with college admissions,

00:04:48.071 --> 00:04:49.855
you take a college applicant,

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.445
you get some set of features about them and some,

00:04:52.446 --> 00:04:53.740
committee make a decision which is in

00:04:53.741 --> 00:04:55.230
part based on a prediction.

00:04:55.231 --> 00:04:56.570
An estimate of their probability

00:04:56.571 --> 00:04:58.330
of succeeding in this program.

00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:00.685
Now, in the offline world,

00:05:00.686 --> 00:05:03.730
these are relatively high stakes decisions compared with

00:05:03.731 --> 00:05:05.820
ranking recommendation where maybe billions of

00:05:05.821 --> 00:05:08.450
low stakes decisions are being made by the algorithm.

00:05:08.451 --> 00:05:10.600
But fundamentally, they all sort of

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:13.170
follow this common pipeline, right?

00:05:13.171 --> 00:05:14.930
We're mapping individual features to

00:05:14.931 --> 00:05:18.790
an algorithm and some sort of predictions being made.

00:05:18.791 --> 00:05:20.170
Here, I think of the prediction

00:05:20.171 --> 00:05:22.130
as maybe a probability estimate.

00:05:22.131 --> 00:05:25.580
I'm going to assign a probability or you,

00:05:25.581 --> 00:05:27.470
about some future behavior.

00:05:27.471 --> 00:05:28.655
Are you going to be in

00:05:28.656 --> 00:05:30.150
the positive class or negative class?

00:05:30.151 --> 00:05:31.420
Are you going to be a yes or a no?

00:05:31.421 --> 00:05:32.810
Right? Are you going to like the movie?

00:05:32.811 --> 00:05:34.290
Are you going to succeed as an employee?

00:05:34.291 --> 00:05:36.635
So forth.

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:38.440
Once we think about this pipeline and

00:05:38.441 --> 00:05:41.000
how it lives both in the online and the offline world,

00:05:41.001 --> 00:05:43.900
there are a lot of questions that people

00:05:43.901 --> 00:05:46.825
have began to ask

00:05:46.826 --> 00:05:49.170
including questions over here on the right hand side.

00:05:49.171 --> 00:05:51.650
Is the right outcome actually being predicted?

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:53.470
When I try introducing an algorithm into

00:05:53.471 --> 00:05:55.540
some complex situation and say,

00:05:55.541 --> 00:05:56.710
"I'm here to make a prediction."

00:05:56.711 --> 00:05:58.690
Are you really making a prediction that's relevant to

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:01.500
the overall decision that the site is trying to optimize?

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:03.170
Similarly, lots of questions

00:06:03.171 --> 00:06:04.550
get asked about this mapping.

00:06:04.551 --> 00:06:07.335
Are we recording the right features about an individual?

00:06:07.336 --> 00:06:10.085
Are those features reflecting

00:06:10.086 --> 00:06:13.785
problems that we have even in the construction phase?

00:06:13.786 --> 00:06:16.260
And finally, related to these first few points,

00:06:16.261 --> 00:06:17.580
this broad question of,

00:06:17.581 --> 00:06:20.290
is the algorithm making fair decisions?

00:06:20.291 --> 00:06:22.030
Are the algorithm decisions fair?

00:06:22.031 --> 00:06:25.020
And this clearly a complex definitional question

00:06:25.021 --> 00:06:27.260
there as to what we mean by fairness.

00:06:27.261 --> 00:06:31.185
And so that's what I want to start from in this talk.

00:06:31.186 --> 00:06:32.860
The question of what would it mean

00:06:32.861 --> 00:06:35.915
for algorithm decisions to be fair?

00:06:35.916 --> 00:06:37.470
And here are a slightly more specific question,

00:06:37.471 --> 00:06:41.325
what would it mean for algorithmic prediction to be fair?

00:06:41.326 --> 00:06:43.140
Particularly, interested here in

00:06:43.141 --> 00:06:44.730
this active prediction and

00:06:44.731 --> 00:06:46.810
assigning an estimate of a probability.

00:06:46.811 --> 00:06:49.010
Now, I've shown you some examples,

00:06:49.011 --> 00:06:52.010
online content, employment education,

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:53.385
all of these have this kind of thing.

00:06:53.386 --> 00:06:55.070
We could run through many other settings

00:06:55.071 --> 00:06:57.950
where some sort of hybrid of a computer and

00:06:57.951 --> 00:07:00.435
a human decision maker are making predictions

00:07:00.436 --> 00:07:03.765
in granting loans and making medical diagnoses.

00:07:03.766 --> 00:07:05.530
Wide range of areas where

00:07:05.531 --> 00:07:07.555
this template actually fits quite well.

00:07:07.556 --> 00:07:09.055
I want to talk about

00:07:09.056 --> 00:07:11.410
the domain that Sendhil and I have been

00:07:11.411 --> 00:07:13.820
focusing on for the past several years

00:07:13.821 --> 00:07:16.130
which is Criminal Justice.

00:07:16.131 --> 00:07:22.035
And we have been working together with Jens Ludwig,

00:07:22.036 --> 00:07:24.775
Jure Leskovec and Himabindu Lakkaraju on

00:07:24.776 --> 00:07:26.470
developing prediction algorithms in

00:07:26.471 --> 00:07:27.630
this domain and trying

00:07:27.631 --> 00:07:30.610
to look for places where human judges might

00:07:30.611 --> 00:07:36.480
be making errors and making sub-optimal decisions.

00:07:36.481 --> 00:07:38.040
And around the time we were doing that,

00:07:38.041 --> 00:07:41.380
this extremely widely circulated article

00:07:41.381 --> 00:07:43.455
by ProPublica came out in summer of 2016,

00:07:43.456 --> 00:07:44.550
gathered a huge amount of attention.

00:07:44.551 --> 00:07:47.230
And it was focused on a risk tool that was

00:07:47.231 --> 00:07:48.590
actually being used in parts

00:07:48.591 --> 00:07:49.990
of the U.S. criminal justice system.

00:07:49.991 --> 00:07:51.875
This tool called COMPAS.

00:07:51.876 --> 00:07:56.970
And, so, what was COMPAS doing?

00:07:56.971 --> 00:07:58.835
COMPAS at a very high level

00:07:58.836 --> 00:08:02.930
was taking features about

00:08:02.931 --> 00:08:04.290
individual defendants and trying

00:08:04.291 --> 00:08:05.415
to assign a level of risk.

00:08:05.416 --> 00:08:07.475
A risk that they would re-offend

00:08:07.476 --> 00:08:09.150
or perhaps within our powers of measurement,

00:08:09.151 --> 00:08:11.300
at least the risk that they would be re-arrested.

00:08:11.301 --> 00:08:13.090
Okay. And so, it was doing

00:08:13.091 --> 00:08:15.185
this sort of a prediction activity.

00:08:15.186 --> 00:08:19.730
And there were some appeared problems with COMPAS.

00:08:19.731 --> 00:08:22.080
And so, ProPublica did this investigation.

00:08:22.081 --> 00:08:24.300
It was actually a very nice example of

00:08:24.301 --> 00:08:26.440
data journalism where the data

00:08:26.441 --> 00:08:27.640
they used to evaluate COMPAS.

00:08:27.641 --> 00:08:29.210
The code they used alteration, they put all of that

00:08:29.211 --> 00:08:31.140
and GitHubs so that people could

00:08:31.141 --> 00:08:34.140
replicate what they were doing and they arrived at

00:08:34.141 --> 00:08:36.430
some relatively striking findings which they reported

00:08:36.431 --> 00:08:40.235
in 36 point front in their article.

00:08:40.236 --> 00:08:42.720
They identified some form

00:08:42.721 --> 00:08:44.560
of bias in the decisions that was made.

00:08:44.561 --> 00:08:46.980
Now, what was the bias that they identified?

00:08:46.981 --> 00:08:49.235
Here, it is sort of summarized in

00:08:49.236 --> 00:08:52.330
two bullet points in stylized form.

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:55.260
And for those who can't quite read about the site,

00:08:55.261 --> 00:08:57.400
I'll read it out for you.

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.650
So, this is a signing level of risk to each defendants.

00:09:01.651 --> 00:09:02.710
But each defendant then goes out in

00:09:02.711 --> 00:09:04.090
the world and either commits,

00:09:04.091 --> 00:09:05.900
either re-offense or not,

00:09:05.901 --> 00:09:07.970
which again relates with our powers.

00:09:07.971 --> 00:09:11.000
The measure means gets re-arrested or not.

00:09:11.001 --> 00:09:12.860
And so, the first finding was

00:09:12.861 --> 00:09:15.290
that African-American defendants who

00:09:15.291 --> 00:09:17.130
didn't subsequently re-offend had

00:09:17.131 --> 00:09:19.515
higher average scores than white defendants,

00:09:19.516 --> 00:09:21.150
who didn't subsequently re-offend.

00:09:21.151 --> 00:09:24.220
In the first of all point was concerning

00:09:24.221 --> 00:09:25.610
because what we're talking about in

00:09:25.611 --> 00:09:27.970
this first bullet is a whole bunch of people,

00:09:27.971 --> 00:09:30.165
none of whom did anything wrong in the future.

00:09:30.166 --> 00:09:31.720
And yet, the average score

00:09:31.721 --> 00:09:32.960
assigned to the African-American defendants in

00:09:32.961 --> 00:09:34.430
that category was higher than what was

00:09:34.431 --> 00:09:36.100
like to the white defendants.

00:09:36.101 --> 00:09:39.230
Conversely, the same thing happens in

00:09:39.231 --> 00:09:41.545
the opposite direction with people who did re-offend.

00:09:41.546 --> 00:09:43.560
White defendants who subsequently re-offended had

00:09:43.561 --> 00:09:45.715
lower average scores than African-American defendants,

00:09:45.716 --> 00:09:47.035
who subsequently re-offended.

00:09:47.036 --> 00:09:51.400
Okay. So, both of these seem like some source of concern.

00:09:51.401 --> 00:09:54.160
And especially, the first bullet is

00:09:54.161 --> 00:09:55.530
a good one to focus on, right?

00:09:55.531 --> 00:09:56.680
People who didn't do

00:09:56.681 --> 00:09:59.590
anything wrong in the future

00:09:59.591 --> 00:10:01.040
and yet we're getting these different scores.

00:10:01.041 --> 00:10:03.430
And usually of the scores assigned to somebody as,

00:10:03.431 --> 00:10:05.050
it incurs a big cost on, right?

00:10:05.051 --> 00:10:06.475
If you get a high score,

00:10:06.476 --> 00:10:09.240
that's now something which has consequences

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:12.560
in how you get treated by the U.S. criminal justices.

00:10:12.561 --> 00:10:15.165
Okay. Yeah. Question?

00:10:15.166 --> 00:10:20.910
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] How do they compute the top score?

00:10:20.911 --> 00:10:23.330
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah. Right. So, this is something that we

00:10:23.331 --> 00:10:26.050
dealt with a lot in the work that we've been doing,

00:10:26.051 --> 00:10:27.810
where if I release

00:10:27.811 --> 00:10:28.650
someone I find out what

00:10:28.651 --> 00:10:30.005
happened if I don't release someone.

00:10:30.006 --> 00:10:31.560
In these particular cases,

00:10:31.561 --> 00:10:33.070
these were four offenses that were going to involve

00:10:33.071 --> 00:10:34.590
relatively short term detention and so they were

00:10:34.591 --> 00:10:35.930
using what happened after they

00:10:35.931 --> 00:10:38.840
were released either immediately or eventually.

00:10:38.841 --> 00:10:43.780
Actually, many issues, one of which is that one,

00:10:43.781 --> 00:10:47.225
make this kind of analysis much more complicated.

00:10:47.226 --> 00:10:50.725
And in the kind of activity that followed this report,

00:10:50.726 --> 00:10:54.020
a lot of the methodology here was question,

00:10:54.021 --> 00:10:55.410
was refined, and so forth.

00:10:55.411 --> 00:10:58.830
Fundamentally, this category of objections was robust.

00:10:58.831 --> 00:11:00.070
In the sense that, for a lot of

00:11:00.071 --> 00:11:01.010
different ways of looking at this,

00:11:01.011 --> 00:11:02.415
this seemed to be happening.

00:11:02.416 --> 00:11:04.720
Yeah. Are there were questions about the. Sir?

00:11:04.721 --> 00:11:07.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, assuming the [inaudible] significant.

00:11:07.201 --> 00:11:08.615
&gt;&gt; Assuming.

00:11:08.616 --> 00:11:10.680
&gt;&gt; So, you're saying the average score,

00:11:10.681 --> 00:11:12.130
I guess, is higher but [inaudible].

00:11:12.131 --> 00:11:13.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:11:13.441 --> 00:11:15.565
There was a relatively non-trivial difference here.

00:11:15.566 --> 00:11:16.000
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:11:16.001 --> 00:11:18.280
&gt;&gt; So.

00:11:18.281 --> 00:11:20.260
&gt;&gt; How do you know that, you know that

00:11:20.261 --> 00:11:21.470
this is just because of

00:11:21.471 --> 00:11:25.170
[inaudible] other factors that would be harder that to.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:28.170
&gt;&gt; The question will be to

00:11:28.171 --> 00:11:29.920
get into like why is this happening right?

00:11:29.921 --> 00:11:31.380
We should be asking why is this happening?

00:11:31.381 --> 00:11:35.090
But, we should remember that in the end,

00:11:35.091 --> 00:11:35.870
if we think about this as

00:11:35.871 --> 00:11:37.160
an activity in the world, right?

00:11:37.161 --> 00:11:40.050
So, when we say it may have to do with the features,

00:11:40.051 --> 00:11:41.010
it may have to do with

00:11:41.011 --> 00:11:42.460
covariance and the feature and so forth,

00:11:42.461 --> 00:11:44.380
that's fine, but that was our decision

00:11:44.381 --> 00:11:46.180
to impose that structure in the situation.

00:11:46.181 --> 00:11:47.950
What's actually happening in the world is

00:11:47.951 --> 00:11:49.355
defendants walk into a courtroom,

00:11:49.356 --> 00:11:50.410
they're assigned scores,

00:11:50.411 --> 00:11:52.570
where high scores impose

00:11:52.571 --> 00:11:54.420
a relatively high cost

00:11:54.421 --> 00:11:56.760
on them and how they're treated in the future.

00:11:56.761 --> 00:11:58.530
Scores get signed to them, they leave

00:11:58.531 --> 00:12:00.490
the courtroom and none of them do anything wrong, right?

00:12:00.491 --> 00:12:01.620
So in the world what's

00:12:01.621 --> 00:12:03.095
happening is people past through the system,

00:12:03.096 --> 00:12:04.560
don't really run in the future, yet we've picked

00:12:04.561 --> 00:12:05.390
this category of people and

00:12:05.391 --> 00:12:06.685
we've assigned them higher scores.

00:12:06.686 --> 00:12:08.630
We could discuss why we're doing that,

00:12:08.631 --> 00:12:10.490
but fundamentally that end-to

00:12:10.491 --> 00:12:12.965
end-process seems like it has something wrong with it.

00:12:12.966 --> 00:12:17.370
And now that was fundamentally their argument.

00:12:17.371 --> 00:12:20.440
So, in the analysis that followed,

00:12:20.441 --> 00:12:21.740
the first thing that

00:12:21.741 --> 00:12:23.635
happened was people said, "All right well,

00:12:23.636 --> 00:12:26.390
if this is an issue with the COMPAS risk tool,

00:12:26.391 --> 00:12:28.270
maybe something- if you

00:12:28.271 --> 00:12:30.435
want to understand what's happening,

00:12:30.436 --> 00:12:31.950
can we understand at least what COMPAS

00:12:31.951 --> 00:12:33.250
was optimized to do?"

00:12:33.251 --> 00:12:35.920
And there actually was an interesting point here,

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:37.505
and this was made both

00:12:37.506 --> 00:12:39.210
by people who were involved with the creation of

00:12:39.211 --> 00:12:40.290
the COMPAS tool and also by

00:12:40.291 --> 00:12:43.700
a collection of researchers in crime policy who

00:12:43.701 --> 00:12:46.120
pointed out that there is another definition

00:12:46.121 --> 00:12:47.160
that we should be thinking about when

00:12:47.161 --> 00:12:48.645
we evaluate risk tools,

00:12:48.646 --> 00:12:50.525
which is not the ones that ProPublica's suggested,

00:12:50.526 --> 00:12:52.370
but rather this notion of calibration.

00:12:52.371 --> 00:12:54.570
And in fact COMPAS's scores

00:12:54.571 --> 00:12:57.470
were well calibrated for each group separately.

00:12:57.471 --> 00:12:59.325
So, what do I mean by calibration?

00:12:59.326 --> 00:13:02.480
So we could think of a risk tool as

00:13:02.481 --> 00:13:04.970
just criticizing the space of risks and so

00:13:04.971 --> 00:13:07.230
it's assigning defendants to these bins.

00:13:07.231 --> 00:13:08.605
This is the 0.1 risk bin,

00:13:08.606 --> 00:13:10.735
0.2, 0.4 and so forth.

00:13:10.736 --> 00:13:11.710
Point one means you only have

00:13:11.711 --> 00:13:13.630
10 percent chance of re-offending.

00:13:13.631 --> 00:13:15.020
And again, whenever I say

00:13:15.021 --> 00:13:16.420
re-offending I mean re-offending in

00:13:16.421 --> 00:13:17.840
the context where you've been arrested

00:13:17.841 --> 00:13:18.720
by the criminal justice system

00:13:18.721 --> 00:13:20.220
which is all that we're measuring.

00:13:20.221 --> 00:13:22.310
And so if it's well

00:13:22.311 --> 00:13:24.510
calibrated to be the case that when I look into this bin,

00:13:24.511 --> 00:13:26.945
one-tenth of all the people there did reoffend.

00:13:26.946 --> 00:13:28.600
Because one-tenth should mean one-tenth.

00:13:28.601 --> 00:13:31.640
And when I look into the 0.2 bin a 0.2

00:13:31.641 --> 00:13:33.235
fraction of the people should

00:13:33.236 --> 00:13:35.605
reoffend because that's what point two means.

00:13:35.606 --> 00:13:37.110
And indeed that is

00:13:37.111 --> 00:13:38.960
the property that the COMPAS scores had.

00:13:38.961 --> 00:13:40.910
And they had it separately for each group.

00:13:40.911 --> 00:13:42.275
It had it for the African-American defendants,

00:13:42.276 --> 00:13:43.850
it had for the white defendants, right?

00:13:43.851 --> 00:13:46.460
So, their point was when we assign

00:13:46.461 --> 00:13:48.140
an African-American defendant a score

00:13:48.141 --> 00:13:50.080
of 0.2, it means 0.2.

00:13:50.081 --> 00:13:52.250
If you were to go and look later 20 percent

00:13:52.251 --> 00:13:53.560
of those people re-offended.

00:13:53.561 --> 00:13:55.380
Point two meant 0.2. If you look

00:13:55.381 --> 00:13:57.390
at the white defense who got assigned a score of 0.2,

00:13:57.391 --> 00:13:59.450
0.2 meant 0.2, right?

00:13:59.451 --> 00:14:02.445
So this in their argument

00:14:02.446 --> 00:14:05.740
there's not much more we can do beyond that promise.

00:14:05.741 --> 00:14:06.990
We promised that the numbers would mean what

00:14:06.991 --> 00:14:09.065
they said, they do mean what they said.

00:14:09.066 --> 00:14:11.420
And adding race into

00:14:11.421 --> 00:14:13.170
this does not differentiate between that

00:14:13.171 --> 00:14:14.840
because it's calibrated separately within

00:14:14.841 --> 00:14:17.415
each group, score of S means the same.

00:14:17.416 --> 00:14:21.575
Now, you could ask well,

00:14:21.576 --> 00:14:24.830
is calibration important I mean it sounds good.

00:14:24.831 --> 00:14:27.160
Is there some societal benefit to having calibration.

00:14:27.161 --> 00:14:30.470
For one thing, it feels somehow

00:14:30.471 --> 00:14:32.650
important that your tools output

00:14:32.651 --> 00:14:34.095
means what it claims to mean,

00:14:34.096 --> 00:14:35.470
but it's also the case that

00:14:35.471 --> 00:14:38.135
uncalibrated rules can induce bad behavior.

00:14:38.136 --> 00:14:40.150
So let me tell a sort

00:14:40.151 --> 00:14:42.400
of fictitious pedagogical story about

00:14:42.401 --> 00:14:44.250
a world in which some entity uses

00:14:44.251 --> 00:14:45.440
an uncalibrated tool and let's

00:14:45.441 --> 00:14:46.830
think about what what might happen, right?

00:14:46.831 --> 00:14:49.840
So I suppose in my fictitious world hospitals run

00:14:49.841 --> 00:14:51.290
a tool to predict

00:14:51.291 --> 00:14:53.670
very accurately how good a doctor you're going to be.

00:14:53.671 --> 00:14:57.200
And they hire candidates by stratifying them into

00:14:57.201 --> 00:14:58.610
bins and only hiring

00:14:58.611 --> 00:15:01.230
the candidate with the very highest score s*.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:05.915
But suppose the rule is not calibrated across groups.

00:15:05.916 --> 00:15:08.090
In particular that would mean something

00:15:08.091 --> 00:15:10.040
like female doctors with the score s* are

00:15:10.041 --> 00:15:11.450
actually more likely to be good doctors than

00:15:11.451 --> 00:15:14.500
male doctors with the same score s*, right?

00:15:14.501 --> 00:15:16.520
S* does not mean s* for the two groups.

00:15:16.521 --> 00:15:18.110
In fact the probably is higher

00:15:18.111 --> 00:15:20.375
for the female doctor who gets s*.

00:15:20.376 --> 00:15:21.660
That would be what it would mean for

00:15:21.661 --> 00:15:23.250
the rule to be uncalibrated.

00:15:23.251 --> 00:15:24.660
Then the point is as

00:15:24.661 --> 00:15:26.200
a patient it would now actually become

00:15:26.201 --> 00:15:27.430
rational for you to choose

00:15:27.431 --> 00:15:29.455
your doctor at least in part based on their gender.

00:15:29.456 --> 00:15:31.140
Gender has now actually become an informative

00:15:31.141 --> 00:15:33.285
feature in your selection of doctors.

00:15:33.286 --> 00:15:35.575
The tool actually is encouraging to do it.

00:15:35.576 --> 00:15:37.465
It says in fact,

00:15:37.466 --> 00:15:39.930
one group actually has a higher probability of being

00:15:39.931 --> 00:15:42.195
a good doctor than the other.

00:15:42.196 --> 00:15:44.495
So even if you're sort of

00:15:44.496 --> 00:15:45.800
imposed self-discipline and choose to

00:15:45.801 --> 00:15:47.175
ignore this failure calibration,

00:15:47.176 --> 00:15:50.190
the downstream people in society may not be doing that.

00:15:50.191 --> 00:15:52.260
So this is one of the styles of

00:15:52.261 --> 00:15:54.740
arguments why you might want calibration across groups.

00:15:54.741 --> 00:15:57.460
Okay. So, calibration is important.

00:15:57.461 --> 00:16:00.210
On the other hand ProPublica's objections still

00:16:00.211 --> 00:16:03.020
remained, right?

00:16:03.021 --> 00:16:05.150
They still seem to the problem. So, the question was,

00:16:05.151 --> 00:16:07.005
could we achieve all of these desired properties?

00:16:07.006 --> 00:16:08.460
We now have multiple properties all of

00:16:08.461 --> 00:16:10.540
which seem to connote fairness.

00:16:10.541 --> 00:16:14.110
And at this point an elaborate argument

00:16:14.111 --> 00:16:17.710
ensued and Sendhil and Minish and I sort

00:16:17.711 --> 00:16:19.000
of watching this felt

00:16:19.001 --> 00:16:21.960
like if we can at least write down the definitions that

00:16:21.961 --> 00:16:23.700
people are arguing about which is so far

00:16:23.701 --> 00:16:25.640
gone sort of only partially

00:16:25.641 --> 00:16:27.200
specified maybe we can actually look at

00:16:27.201 --> 00:16:28.650
the situation try to figure

00:16:28.651 --> 00:16:30.685
out what the argument is actually about.

00:16:30.686 --> 00:16:33.740
And so, this next few slides is

00:16:33.741 --> 00:16:36.070
really about something which

00:16:36.071 --> 00:16:38.490
mathematically ends up not being that hard.

00:16:38.491 --> 00:16:40.240
The challenge is to figure out what

00:16:40.241 --> 00:16:43.320
is the sort of definitional content of this argument.

00:16:43.321 --> 00:16:45.545
So, here's what we're

00:16:45.546 --> 00:16:47.175
talking about in terms of our model.

00:16:47.176 --> 00:16:48.630
So the basic model for assigning

00:16:48.631 --> 00:16:50.510
risk scores is probability estimates

00:16:50.511 --> 00:16:52.000
is that I have individuals.

00:16:52.001 --> 00:16:54.010
They each have two things that are salient,

00:16:54.011 --> 00:16:55.395
they are either positive or negative, right?

00:16:55.396 --> 00:16:56.920
They will reoffend or they won't reoffend,

00:16:56.921 --> 00:16:57.680
they'll be a good employee or

00:16:57.681 --> 00:16:58.735
they won't be a good employee.

00:16:58.736 --> 00:16:59.840
There's some kind of notion of

00:16:59.841 --> 00:17:01.625
the positive class, the negative class.

00:17:01.626 --> 00:17:04.210
And they also belong to one of two groups, right?

00:17:04.211 --> 00:17:05.970
Two groups where I'm interested in the imbalance

00:17:05.971 --> 00:17:08.630
between them or lack thereof. Groups A or B.

00:17:08.631 --> 00:17:10.920
The in focus maps the feature vector and then

00:17:10.921 --> 00:17:15.195
my risk score maps them to one of a set of bins,

00:17:15.196 --> 00:17:17.090
bin B has a score of Visa B which

00:17:17.091 --> 00:17:19.370
is sort of the probability estimate to do that.

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:22.460
Okay. So and that's

00:17:22.461 --> 00:17:24.635
fundamentally what a risk tool is doing.

00:17:24.636 --> 00:17:26.025
What are the properties that I

00:17:26.026 --> 00:17:27.890
would like from my risk tool.

00:17:27.891 --> 00:17:29.220
Well, here are the

00:17:29.221 --> 00:17:30.515
three that we've been talking about so far.

00:17:30.516 --> 00:17:31.990
Calibration within groups means

00:17:31.991 --> 00:17:33.510
that for each group A and B,

00:17:33.511 --> 00:17:35.740
A visa B fraction of people in bin b

00:17:35.741 --> 00:17:39.020
are in fact positive.

00:17:39.021 --> 00:17:41.250
Then I have these

00:17:41.251 --> 00:17:42.750
issues with the ProPublica raised, right?

00:17:42.751 --> 00:17:45.250
These definitions which really hadn't

00:17:45.251 --> 00:17:47.660
necessarily been as much on the radar in

00:17:47.661 --> 00:17:48.950
the discussion about these

00:17:48.951 --> 00:17:50.725
things until the report came out.

00:17:50.726 --> 00:17:52.910
So, on the positive classroom

00:17:52.911 --> 00:17:54.440
I asked for what I might call balance.

00:17:54.441 --> 00:17:56.730
That the average score of the positive members in

00:17:56.731 --> 00:17:57.880
group A should equal

00:17:57.881 --> 00:17:59.840
the average score the positive members in group B.

00:17:59.841 --> 00:18:01.130
And similarly I might want

00:18:01.131 --> 00:18:02.300
balance for the negative class.

00:18:02.301 --> 00:18:03.070
So the average score of

00:18:03.071 --> 00:18:04.160
negative members in group A should

00:18:04.161 --> 00:18:04.990
be equal the average score of

00:18:04.991 --> 00:18:06.765
negative members in group B.

00:18:06.766 --> 00:18:11.410
Three properties that I might want.

00:18:11.411 --> 00:18:13.210
All of which seemed to connote some kind

00:18:13.211 --> 00:18:15.080
of fairness in the system.

00:18:15.081 --> 00:18:18.105
Okay. So, let's ask

00:18:18.106 --> 00:18:21.205
how might we try to achieve all of these properties?

00:18:21.206 --> 00:18:23.390
Well, let's first try out

00:18:23.391 --> 00:18:25.730
some simple cases where we can achieve them.

00:18:25.731 --> 00:18:28.395
The first case would be, if I have

00:18:28.396 --> 00:18:33.275
perfect prediction which is a really extreme case,

00:18:33.276 --> 00:18:35.460
then I can actually achieve all three properties, right?

00:18:35.461 --> 00:18:38.310
If the features in my data are actually enough that I can

00:18:38.311 --> 00:18:39.880
deterministically tell if someone's in

00:18:39.881 --> 00:18:41.805
the positive class or the negative class,

00:18:41.806 --> 00:18:43.630
then when I'm deterministically sure as

00:18:43.631 --> 00:18:45.590
some of the positive class I give them a score of one,

00:18:45.591 --> 00:18:46.760
when I'm deterministically sure there

00:18:46.761 --> 00:18:47.700
are in the negative class I give them

00:18:47.701 --> 00:18:49.810
a score of zero and in fact

00:18:49.811 --> 00:18:53.820
I'll achieve all three properties you can check that.

00:18:53.821 --> 00:18:54.690
&gt;&gt; Can you go back to the slide.

00:18:54.691 --> 00:18:54.985
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:18:54.986 --> 00:18:58.620
&gt;&gt; So how do you get balance to a positive class?

00:18:58.621 --> 00:19:01.565
&gt;&gt; So, the average score of

00:19:01.566 --> 00:19:03.085
positive members and the average score

00:19:03.086 --> 00:19:05.495
of it all [inaudible] equals one in that case.

00:19:05.496 --> 00:19:07.540
As in if all members of the positive class.

00:19:07.541 --> 00:19:09.695
&gt;&gt; Given your positive the average score was-

00:19:09.696 --> 00:19:12.270
&gt;&gt; Exactly. Given the positive.

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:15.750
Yeah. So in the case of

00:19:15.751 --> 00:19:16.910
ProPublica prediction which we don't

00:19:16.911 --> 00:19:19.105
expect to have, we could achieve this.

00:19:19.106 --> 00:19:22.890
A second case maybe a more plausible case where we

00:19:22.891 --> 00:19:26.135
could also achieve this would be equal base rates.

00:19:26.136 --> 00:19:28.170
Suppose the two groups have the same fraction of

00:19:28.171 --> 00:19:31.150
positive instances some number p. Well,

00:19:31.151 --> 00:19:32.400
then at least there always is

00:19:32.401 --> 00:19:34.800
a risk score that satisfies all the properties.

00:19:34.801 --> 00:19:37.605
I simply assign a probability P to everybody.

00:19:37.606 --> 00:19:39.700
I create one bin, I put everybody into it and they

00:19:39.701 --> 00:19:41.965
get a score of p. Now,

00:19:41.966 --> 00:19:43.520
clearly that satisfies balance because

00:19:43.521 --> 00:19:45.770
everybody's average is p. And also satisfies

00:19:45.771 --> 00:19:48.240
calibration because there's a single bin

00:19:48.241 --> 00:19:50.075
and a p fraction people in their given.

00:19:50.076 --> 00:19:52.600
So in that case, I could at least get a risk score.

00:19:52.601 --> 00:19:54.210
Maybe in the case of equal base rates

00:19:54.211 --> 00:19:55.500
if there's some structure in the problem I

00:19:55.501 --> 00:19:57.110
could create another solution

00:19:57.111 --> 00:19:59.170
that's a more non-trivial risk or with multiple bins.

00:19:59.171 --> 00:20:01.875
But the very least I can satisfy all three properties.

00:20:01.876 --> 00:20:05.265
Okay. So we now have sort of two endpoints here.

00:20:05.266 --> 00:20:07.270
And so we thought about, okay how can we sort of

00:20:07.271 --> 00:20:09.160
work our way in from these two extremes?

00:20:09.161 --> 00:20:11.735
And the sort of

00:20:11.736 --> 00:20:13.690
first main thing I want to

00:20:13.691 --> 00:20:15.895
talk about is that you can't actually.

00:20:15.896 --> 00:20:17.290
So, in any instance of

00:20:17.291 --> 00:20:19.160
risk score assignment where all three properties can be

00:20:19.161 --> 00:20:20.710
achieved you have to either have

00:20:20.711 --> 00:20:23.445
perfect prediction or equal base rates.

00:20:23.446 --> 00:20:25.320
These are the only two situations in which

00:20:25.321 --> 00:20:27.800
you can actually have all three of those properties.

00:20:27.801 --> 00:20:30.030
So I want to walk through why

00:20:30.031 --> 00:20:31.560
that's true, because as I said,

00:20:31.561 --> 00:20:34.170
the proof's in the section of the talk are

00:20:34.171 --> 00:20:36.940
actually quite easy in some sense

00:20:36.941 --> 00:20:39.980
our challenge had been to sort of unravel what was being

00:20:39.981 --> 00:20:41.160
talked about and try to find out what

00:20:41.161 --> 00:20:43.330
the definitions were that people were arguing.

00:20:43.331 --> 00:20:45.275
Once you do that it's

00:20:45.276 --> 00:20:46.830
not that hard to see what's going on.

00:20:46.831 --> 00:20:48.230
And in particular, I should

00:20:48.231 --> 00:20:50.640
mention a couple of things about this, right?

00:20:50.641 --> 00:20:52.580
The way we've defined things

00:20:52.581 --> 00:20:54.130
it gets us to a point where this is not

00:20:54.131 --> 00:20:55.150
a theorem it's about

00:20:55.151 --> 00:20:57.875
computational power or inferential power,

00:20:57.876 --> 00:21:00.770
it's really just about your inability to

00:21:00.771 --> 00:21:04.170
assign numbers to entities

00:21:04.171 --> 00:21:06.960
to equalize certain averages, right?

00:21:06.961 --> 00:21:08.480
So, as a result it's not

00:21:08.481 --> 00:21:10.425
really about the limitations of algorithms.

00:21:10.426 --> 00:21:11.460
Any decision making procedure

00:21:11.461 --> 00:21:12.440
that attempts to assign numbers

00:21:12.441 --> 00:21:13.330
to people is going to

00:21:13.331 --> 00:21:15.325
fail on one of these properties.Yeah.

00:21:15.326 --> 00:21:17.670
&gt;&gt; So this score

00:21:17.671 --> 00:21:19.760
is the previous score that you mentioned that.

00:21:19.761 --> 00:21:21.050
&gt;&gt; No so actually.

00:21:21.051 --> 00:21:21.780
&gt;&gt; Have equal base rates?

00:21:21.781 --> 00:21:23.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah. If you have equal base rates

00:21:23.431 --> 00:21:25.210
it's going to depend on the instance.

00:21:25.211 --> 00:21:28.350
So in fact a slide that I don't have here if

00:21:28.351 --> 00:21:29.670
I give you an instance of this problem

00:21:29.671 --> 00:21:31.100
with equal base rates and I ask,

00:21:31.101 --> 00:21:32.520
does there exist a non-trivial score that's

00:21:32.521 --> 00:21:35.445
actually np hard to figure out if there is or isn't.

00:21:35.446 --> 00:21:37.100
Because there's sort of a knapsack

00:21:37.101 --> 00:21:38.500
like activity that you are subsets on

00:21:38.501 --> 00:21:39.690
like activity you would have to do and

00:21:39.691 --> 00:21:41.555
get a non-trivial one.

00:21:41.556 --> 00:21:46.080
So yes. So it's going to be instance dependent.

00:21:46.081 --> 00:21:50.150
Prediction for one positive,

00:21:50.151 --> 00:21:52.125
one negative in both groups,

00:21:52.126 --> 00:21:53.920
can you sacrifice scores on

00:21:53.921 --> 00:21:56.150
those people who balance it out?

00:21:56.151 --> 00:21:57.910
Suppose we know that somebody is positive,

00:21:57.911 --> 00:22:01.485
you can assign an any positive score above threshold.

00:22:01.486 --> 00:22:02.830
Somebody is negative for any

00:22:02.831 --> 00:22:04.410
negative score below threshold.

00:22:04.411 --> 00:22:06.620
&gt;&gt; I see. Sorry, are you proposing

00:22:06.621 --> 00:22:08.140
a change in the requirements

00:22:08.141 --> 00:22:09.585
or are you proposing a method to?

00:22:09.586 --> 00:22:12.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah, method of solution.

00:22:12.411 --> 00:22:15.100
If you know one through than the one through negative.

00:22:15.101 --> 00:22:16.350
&gt;&gt; If I have equal base

00:22:16.351 --> 00:22:17.560
rates that's how I could start to try

00:22:17.561 --> 00:22:20.720
finding a non-trivial assignment of risk scores.

00:22:20.721 --> 00:22:23.085
I could sort of locally search for it and that's right.

00:22:23.086 --> 00:22:25.030
If I have unequal base rates as I'm going to

00:22:25.031 --> 00:22:26.990
argue, we're not actually going do it.

00:22:26.991 --> 00:22:27.975
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:27.976 --> 00:22:32.130
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, this was

00:22:32.131 --> 00:22:33.855
interesting to us because

00:22:33.856 --> 00:22:36.265
it somehow said that the crux of this debate was that,

00:22:36.266 --> 00:22:38.735
you really can't achieve everything

00:22:38.736 --> 00:22:40.890
that people want to

00:22:40.891 --> 00:22:42.030
unless you're in one of

00:22:42.031 --> 00:22:43.640
these situations and fundamentally saying,

00:22:43.641 --> 00:22:45.580
once you have unequal base rates,

00:22:45.581 --> 00:22:47.110
there's a problem, right?

00:22:47.111 --> 00:22:47.830
There's a problem if

00:22:47.831 --> 00:22:49.415
these two groups have unequal base rates.

00:22:49.416 --> 00:22:50.970
And sort of no amount of

00:22:50.971 --> 00:22:52.860
clever authoring of definitions

00:22:52.861 --> 00:22:54.035
is going to really make that go away.

00:22:54.036 --> 00:22:56.240
It's going to show up somewhere.

00:22:56.241 --> 00:23:00.800
So, we went back to talk to ProPublica half a year later,

00:23:00.801 --> 00:23:02.235
because they generate a lot of activity.

00:23:02.236 --> 00:23:03.880
And a number of groups actually

00:23:03.881 --> 00:23:05.560
we're thinking about similar sorts of questions,

00:23:05.561 --> 00:23:07.410
and so these all got summarized in

00:23:07.411 --> 00:23:09.440
a kind of year-end wrap up that they wrote.

00:23:09.441 --> 00:23:11.330
Coming back to this question and saying that in

00:23:11.331 --> 00:23:13.640
fact the situation was sort of,

00:23:13.641 --> 00:23:15.750
it wasn't just about the data that they had collected.

00:23:15.751 --> 00:23:18.360
There were certain mathematical issues going on.

00:23:18.361 --> 00:23:19.850
Some of the other work that

00:23:19.851 --> 00:23:22.055
got summarized in all of this.

00:23:22.056 --> 00:23:24.720
There was some nice work concurrent with

00:23:24.721 --> 00:23:27.500
ours by Alexandra Chouldechova at Carnegie Mellon,

00:23:27.501 --> 00:23:30.090
looking at a similar setting except

00:23:30.091 --> 00:23:31.380
where the algorithm is supposed

00:23:31.381 --> 00:23:32.810
to output a hard decision,

00:23:32.811 --> 00:23:35.410
not a probably estimate but a yes- no decision and now I

00:23:35.411 --> 00:23:38.185
ask about false positive rates and false negative rates.

00:23:38.186 --> 00:23:40.280
And there's a similar negative result about

00:23:40.281 --> 00:23:41.810
equalizing certain sets of

00:23:41.811 --> 00:23:43.705
false positives and false negative rates.

00:23:43.706 --> 00:23:46.030
Similar results by a group

00:23:46.031 --> 00:23:48.225
at Stanford Corbett Davies et al.

00:23:48.226 --> 00:23:50.390
Some nice work of Moritz Hardt,

00:23:50.391 --> 00:23:52.660
Eric Price, and Nathan Srebro,

00:23:52.661 --> 00:23:54.090
who looked at the case in which

00:23:54.091 --> 00:23:57.260
you take away calibration,

00:23:57.261 --> 00:23:59.595
which, again, comes at some cost.

00:23:59.596 --> 00:24:01.380
But then, we could

00:24:01.381 --> 00:24:03.220
look at a constrained optimization problem,

00:24:03.221 --> 00:24:04.840
how could I be as accurate as possible if I want

00:24:04.841 --> 00:24:06.600
to equalize false positive rates,

00:24:06.601 --> 00:24:08.070
equal false negative rates.

00:24:08.071 --> 00:24:10.580
Or, in our terms impose balance for

00:24:10.581 --> 00:24:12.130
the positive-negative classes and then

00:24:12.131 --> 00:24:14.125
try to be as accurate as possible,

00:24:14.126 --> 00:24:17.100
which will necessarily typically sacrifice calibration.

00:24:17.101 --> 00:24:20.140
We also in a paper nips this past year

00:24:20.141 --> 00:24:23.055
looked at some other versions of this where for example,

00:24:23.056 --> 00:24:24.870
if I want to retain calibration,

00:24:24.871 --> 00:24:27.660
I can basically equalize any kind of

00:24:27.661 --> 00:24:29.200
one dimensional combination of

00:24:29.201 --> 00:24:31.050
the balance for the positive-negative classes

00:24:31.051 --> 00:24:33.125
but not both simultaneously.

00:24:33.126 --> 00:24:35.870
Let me give you a quick sketch of, yeah, question.

00:24:35.871 --> 00:24:38.430
&gt;&gt; In the change of requirements,

00:24:38.431 --> 00:24:40.300
is there any limits on the bias

00:24:40.301 --> 00:24:42.450
that we can work towards to so I can better know?.

00:24:42.451 --> 00:24:44.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah, right. It's a good question.

00:24:44.051 --> 00:24:45.770
So can we get some approximate version.

00:24:45.771 --> 00:24:48.460
So, if I write down a notion of

00:24:48.461 --> 00:24:51.250
approximation in a really sort of simple way,

00:24:51.251 --> 00:24:52.550
I say, I would like

00:24:52.551 --> 00:24:54.270
approximate calibration to with an Epsilon.

00:24:54.271 --> 00:24:56.810
I'd like equalizing averages within Epsilon and I'd like

00:24:56.811 --> 00:25:01.370
equalizing balance cost negative within Epsilon, then no.

00:25:01.371 --> 00:25:03.460
In fact, basically it says you have to have

00:25:03.461 --> 00:25:05.380
either approximately perfect prediction or

00:25:05.381 --> 00:25:06.760
approximately equal base rates and

00:25:06.761 --> 00:25:09.020
that's the only situation.

00:25:09.021 --> 00:25:11.540
Other forms of approximation I think are sort of in

00:25:11.541 --> 00:25:13.935
these lower bullets here where it's basically saying,

00:25:13.936 --> 00:25:15.250
we may have to just wholesale

00:25:15.251 --> 00:25:16.655
give up on one of these conditions.

00:25:16.656 --> 00:25:20.330
And I think part of the message here is going to be that,

00:25:20.331 --> 00:25:22.385
which condition you want to give up.

00:25:22.386 --> 00:25:24.560
I'm going to argue as a sort of

00:25:24.561 --> 00:25:28.425
non-technical point is going to be domain dependent.

00:25:28.426 --> 00:25:30.530
And there are certain extremes, right.?

00:25:30.531 --> 00:25:33.970
There are situations where the societal cost of

00:25:33.971 --> 00:25:35.530
having unequal averages for

00:25:35.531 --> 00:25:37.775
people who committed no crime for example,

00:25:37.776 --> 00:25:40.400
we view as very high and that's what we want to focus on,

00:25:40.401 --> 00:25:42.855
and if we loosen calibration, so be it.

00:25:42.856 --> 00:25:46.500
There are other domains where giving

00:25:46.501 --> 00:25:47.580
up on calibration for the sake of

00:25:47.581 --> 00:25:49.920
balance seems to make very little sense.

00:25:49.921 --> 00:25:52.075
So, for example, take medical,

00:25:52.076 --> 00:25:55.035
take a sort of hypothetical medical diagnosis setting,

00:25:55.036 --> 00:25:58.440
where I have a rare disease,

00:25:58.441 --> 00:25:59.690
do some test for it,

00:25:59.691 --> 00:26:01.310
which returns with some probability.

00:26:01.311 --> 00:26:03.390
And the disease for

00:26:03.391 --> 00:26:06.085
some genuine genetic or physiological reason

00:26:06.086 --> 00:26:08.255
say is more common in men than women.

00:26:08.256 --> 00:26:10.610
Okay. So, the base rates are unequal.

00:26:10.611 --> 00:26:13.540
So now the risk score

00:26:13.541 --> 00:26:15.600
for that disease fits in our framework.

00:26:15.601 --> 00:26:17.165
You take the features of the person,

00:26:17.166 --> 00:26:18.750
you output or probably that they

00:26:18.751 --> 00:26:20.170
have the disease based on what you measured.

00:26:20.171 --> 00:26:21.310
It could be high, it could be low based

00:26:21.311 --> 00:26:22.810
on various tests that you ran.

00:26:22.811 --> 00:26:24.740
But because the base rates are unequal and you

00:26:24.741 --> 00:26:26.820
have imperfect prediction and

00:26:26.821 --> 00:26:29.980
you're going to have some disparity

00:26:29.981 --> 00:26:32.200
across men and women, right?

00:26:32.201 --> 00:26:36.510
There it feels like the fact that the average for

00:26:36.511 --> 00:26:38.820
the positive class negative class are unequal is somehow

00:26:38.821 --> 00:26:41.370
a feature of just the nature of the disease, right?

00:26:41.371 --> 00:26:43.590
It's not necessary reflecting something wrong in

00:26:43.591 --> 00:26:45.750
society relative just a fact about this disease.

00:26:45.751 --> 00:26:47.300
Whereas having a rule that's uncalibrated,

00:26:47.301 --> 00:26:48.340
in that case, it would be strange.

00:26:48.341 --> 00:26:49.480
It would mean you would go

00:26:49.481 --> 00:26:50.710
to the doctor, and the doctor would say,

00:26:50.711 --> 00:26:52.005
"Well we looked at some features

00:26:52.006 --> 00:26:54.650
and this thing reports a 20 percent chance,

00:26:54.651 --> 00:26:56.140
but because you're male, we have to adjust

00:26:56.141 --> 00:26:57.915
that because it's an uncalibrated."

00:26:57.916 --> 00:26:59.580
It's somehow wouldn't necessarily

00:26:59.581 --> 00:27:00.885
make sense to go to calibration in that case.

00:27:00.886 --> 00:27:02.410
So, I think which one you want to give

00:27:02.411 --> 00:27:04.480
up is going to be domain-dependent

00:27:04.481 --> 00:27:06.400
and it's going depend on what we view as

00:27:06.401 --> 00:27:09.880
the relative cost of these different forms of unfairness.

00:27:09.881 --> 00:27:11.980
Let me just mention two slides

00:27:11.981 --> 00:27:14.060
about the proof just to kind of emphasize that

00:27:14.061 --> 00:27:17.190
it's really not very

00:27:17.191 --> 00:27:19.265
complicated at all, once we have these definitions.

00:27:19.266 --> 00:27:23.570
If you want to watch for something in the proof,

00:27:23.571 --> 00:27:24.280
it might be the following,

00:27:24.281 --> 00:27:26.150
which is somewhere in the proof of course.

00:27:26.151 --> 00:27:27.360
We're going to have to mention perfect

00:27:27.361 --> 00:27:28.430
prediction equal base rates.

00:27:28.431 --> 00:27:30.600
Because the theorem is not, you can never do this.

00:27:30.601 --> 00:27:32.410
The theorem is you can only do it in these two cases.

00:27:32.411 --> 00:27:34.010
And these two cases are kind of a combination

00:27:34.011 --> 00:27:35.885
of like a zero dimensional case,

00:27:35.886 --> 00:27:37.850
where things are pinned down to constants and

00:27:37.851 --> 00:27:38.980
kind of a one dimensional case

00:27:38.981 --> 00:27:39.800
where two things are equalized.

00:27:39.801 --> 00:27:42.510
So, somewhere in the proof, short though it is,

00:27:42.511 --> 00:27:43.820
it's going to have to be kind of

00:27:43.821 --> 00:27:45.360
a zero dimensional thing glued to

00:27:45.361 --> 00:27:47.230
a one dimensional thing that's going to somehow say

00:27:47.231 --> 00:27:49.730
these are the two cases. So watch for that.

00:27:49.731 --> 00:27:51.525
Okay. So we have these groups,

00:27:51.526 --> 00:27:53.930
let's say N sub t is the number of people in group t,

00:27:53.931 --> 00:27:55.790
K sub t is the expected number of people in

00:27:55.791 --> 00:27:57.940
the positive class in group t. And I'm sort

00:27:57.941 --> 00:27:59.830
of speaking informally and all of

00:27:59.831 --> 00:28:00.800
this takes a little

00:28:00.801 --> 00:28:02.025
more to write this down more carefully.

00:28:02.026 --> 00:28:04.650
So the calibration condition, if you think about it says,

00:28:04.651 --> 00:28:07.560
it has been one out of 10 people commit a crime.

00:28:07.561 --> 00:28:09.490
And we have 10 people assigned the score of point one,

00:28:09.491 --> 00:28:10.830
which is a total score of one.

00:28:10.831 --> 00:28:14.540
Similarly here we've assigned 10 copies of point two,

00:28:14.541 --> 00:28:15.620
which is a total score of two

00:28:15.621 --> 00:28:16.825
and two people commit a crime.

00:28:16.826 --> 00:28:18.480
Fundamentally, the calibration is saying,

00:28:18.481 --> 00:28:20.870
the total score handed out in the bin is equal to

00:28:20.871 --> 00:28:22.150
the expected number of people in that bin

00:28:22.151 --> 00:28:23.650
who actually are in the positive class.

00:28:23.651 --> 00:28:25.810
And something overall bins that basically says,

00:28:25.811 --> 00:28:27.225
the total score handed out to all of

00:28:27.226 --> 00:28:29.490
group t is going to be

00:28:29.491 --> 00:28:31.245
the expected number of positive people

00:28:31.246 --> 00:28:33.440
in group t. All right.

00:28:33.441 --> 00:28:36.680
So let's remember that. Okay. Let's say

00:28:36.681 --> 00:28:37.920
we have the average score of a person in

00:28:37.921 --> 00:28:39.400
the negative class, we'll call that X.

00:28:39.401 --> 00:28:40.130
The average score of a person in

00:28:40.131 --> 00:28:41.940
the positive class we'll call that Y.

00:28:41.941 --> 00:28:44.120
The key is because we've equalized the averages that's

00:28:44.121 --> 00:28:45.150
independent of the group we're talking

00:28:45.151 --> 00:28:47.050
about. That's crucial.

00:28:47.051 --> 00:28:49.540
So here's a way to write the total score in

00:28:49.541 --> 00:28:54.060
group t. nt minus kt people were in the negative class,

00:28:54.061 --> 00:28:55.465
they get a score of X on average.

00:28:55.466 --> 00:28:56.750
Kt are in the positive class,

00:28:56.751 --> 00:28:58.220
they get score of Y on average.

00:28:58.221 --> 00:28:59.010
On the other hand,

00:28:59.011 --> 00:29:00.530
we know that the total score handed out as

00:29:00.531 --> 00:29:03.330
Kt by calibration. So I get this equation.

00:29:03.331 --> 00:29:04.610
So now I have two equations,

00:29:04.611 --> 00:29:07.220
one for the first group, one for the second group.

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:09.940
Each of these equations specifies

00:29:09.941 --> 00:29:12.290
some line relating X and Y.

00:29:12.291 --> 00:29:14.050
Here are those two lines.

00:29:14.051 --> 00:29:15.825
And now the question is,

00:29:15.826 --> 00:29:17.760
when can those be simultaneously satisfied?

00:29:17.761 --> 00:29:19.140
Well, one is at the point

00:29:19.141 --> 00:29:20.710
zero one, which is perfect prediction.

00:29:20.711 --> 00:29:23.840
And the other is if these two slopes happen to be equal,

00:29:23.841 --> 00:29:25.090
in which case, they coincide

00:29:25.091 --> 00:29:26.250
all the way along these lines,

00:29:26.251 --> 00:29:28.065
which is the case of equal base rates.

00:29:28.066 --> 00:29:30.130
So, fundamentally that's what's going on.

00:29:30.131 --> 00:29:31.310
And we have to be a little

00:29:31.311 --> 00:29:32.560
more careful in how we write everything down.

00:29:32.561 --> 00:29:34.350
But that's basically.

00:29:34.351 --> 00:29:39.850
Okay. So we looked at this and we thought they sort of,

00:29:39.851 --> 00:29:43.110
some interesting thing is going on here.

00:29:43.111 --> 00:29:45.370
And we began thinking about the ways in which probably

00:29:45.371 --> 00:29:47.900
estimates are being used more broadly.

00:29:47.901 --> 00:29:49.400
So we thought about that this

00:29:49.401 --> 00:29:51.400
is sort of one first look at

00:29:51.401 --> 00:29:52.930
this activity of taking

00:29:52.931 --> 00:29:55.230
people and making probabilistic estimates about them,

00:29:55.231 --> 00:29:58.155
and then making decisions based on that.

00:29:58.156 --> 00:30:00.435
But many of those situations

00:30:00.436 --> 00:30:02.400
involve more than just one individual, right.

00:30:02.401 --> 00:30:05.480
What is interesting here is that,

00:30:05.481 --> 00:30:07.150
we're auditing the rule.

00:30:07.151 --> 00:30:09.680
We're evaluating it by looking at averages over groups,

00:30:09.681 --> 00:30:11.140
but the rule, itself, is being applied

00:30:11.141 --> 00:30:12.405
one individual at a time.

00:30:12.406 --> 00:30:14.575
Fundamentally, the activity is

00:30:14.576 --> 00:30:16.730
the risk score takes in a single individual and

00:30:16.731 --> 00:30:18.940
outputs a single number and there are

00:30:18.941 --> 00:30:20.180
many cases when we assign

00:30:20.181 --> 00:30:21.615
problems to get estimates about people.

00:30:21.616 --> 00:30:24.720
What we're really doing is evaluating a group, right?

00:30:24.721 --> 00:30:26.330
We're applying a function to a set not to

00:30:26.331 --> 00:30:28.260
an individual. What do I mean by that?

00:30:28.261 --> 00:30:29.630
Well, we all sort

00:30:29.631 --> 00:30:32.040
of encounter that on a regular basis, right?

00:30:32.041 --> 00:30:35.065
So just to take a sort of representative example.

00:30:35.066 --> 00:30:37.270
A couple of years ago there was this headline

00:30:37.271 --> 00:30:38.600
during college admissions season

00:30:38.601 --> 00:30:39.550
in the Cornell Chronicle,

00:30:39.551 --> 00:30:42.545
which is our local on-campus news,

00:30:42.546 --> 00:30:44.340
which says, "The admitted class of 2019 is the

00:30:44.341 --> 00:30:46.435
most diverse in Cornell history."

00:30:46.436 --> 00:30:48.080
That's a statement about a group,

00:30:48.081 --> 00:30:49.350
saying about the admitted class, right?

00:30:49.351 --> 00:30:52.705
No one individual is the most diverse in Cornell history.

00:30:52.706 --> 00:30:54.450
It's really what happens when you bring together a group

00:30:54.451 --> 00:30:56.385
of people and evaluate their diversity as a set.

00:30:56.386 --> 00:30:58.080
I'm evaluating sets.

00:30:58.081 --> 00:31:00.610
And this was really a point

00:31:00.611 --> 00:31:03.310
brought to the fore by the work of Scott Page,

00:31:03.311 --> 00:31:05.360
this social scientist at the University of Michigan,

00:31:05.361 --> 00:31:06.710
who does very interesting work.

00:31:06.711 --> 00:31:10.420
And has written for a number of years on the issue of

00:31:10.421 --> 00:31:12.880
diversity as a sort of emergent property of

00:31:12.881 --> 00:31:16.385
groups and the performance benefits of diversity.

00:31:16.386 --> 00:31:18.170
And college admissions is one of

00:31:18.171 --> 00:31:20.250
the examples which he thinks about.

00:31:20.251 --> 00:31:21.540
But of course, there are a number of other

00:31:21.541 --> 00:31:23.600
settings when we think about this.

00:31:23.601 --> 00:31:25.500
Often missing in the discussion of

00:31:25.501 --> 00:31:27.990
assignments or loans for example, right.

00:31:27.991 --> 00:31:30.110
We think people making loan decisions is that in

00:31:30.111 --> 00:31:32.800
the end you have to evaluate loans collectively.

00:31:32.801 --> 00:31:34.290
Right. You're really valuing the portfolio of

00:31:34.291 --> 00:31:36.900
loans not anyone individually.

00:31:36.901 --> 00:31:39.310
Similarly, a bunch of

00:31:39.311 --> 00:31:41.215
work starting with work of Scott page,

00:31:41.216 --> 00:31:42.740
and then some stuff is going to

00:31:42.741 --> 00:31:44.080
work that might Rod and I do,

00:31:44.081 --> 00:31:45.590
which I'll mention toward the end.

00:31:45.591 --> 00:31:47.340
Looking at for example, if I wanted to select

00:31:47.341 --> 00:31:49.500
a team to maximize team performance,

00:31:49.501 --> 00:31:51.590
I should be evaluating the team not necessarily

00:31:51.591 --> 00:31:54.500
the individuals on the team.

00:31:54.501 --> 00:31:55.950
So you end up with

00:31:55.951 --> 00:31:58.040
this challenge in which we're trying to evaluate

00:31:58.041 --> 00:31:59.800
a group by making probabilistic estimates about

00:31:59.801 --> 00:32:02.050
the individuals and what's going to happen there.

00:32:02.051 --> 00:32:05.280
So, it means Rod and I are having thought about

00:32:05.281 --> 00:32:07.500
this assignment of risk score has began

00:32:07.501 --> 00:32:09.880
thinking about this in the context of group selection,

00:32:09.881 --> 00:32:12.605
and asked what are some of the issues that come up there?

00:32:12.606 --> 00:32:16.690
And we came across this interesting thing which really

00:32:16.691 --> 00:32:22.235
hadn't been sort of analyzed mathematically.

00:32:22.236 --> 00:32:24.875
We've had some interesting properties.

00:32:24.876 --> 00:32:27.030
So again to kind of recap what

00:32:27.031 --> 00:32:29.785
we kind of a generic situation that we're thinking about.

00:32:29.786 --> 00:32:31.290
We have a large pool of applicants,

00:32:31.291 --> 00:32:33.025
we'd like to choose a short list of K.

00:32:33.026 --> 00:32:35.395
So maybe that's our entering college class.

00:32:35.396 --> 00:32:37.130
Maybe, I guess the canonical example

00:32:37.131 --> 00:32:38.870
might be these are the job applicants and we're going to

00:32:38.871 --> 00:32:40.175
select K to kind of

00:32:40.176 --> 00:32:42.230
interview or sort of look extra hard at.

00:32:42.231 --> 00:32:43.420
Okay. And we'd like

00:32:43.421 --> 00:32:45.985
this group of K to be as good as possible.

00:32:45.986 --> 00:32:49.880
Now the concern is that there's bias in the system,

00:32:49.881 --> 00:32:53.475
either explicit a bias or maybe implicit bias such that,

00:32:53.476 --> 00:32:55.370
maybe group X is

00:32:55.371 --> 00:32:56.210
a group where we're concerned there's

00:32:56.211 --> 00:32:57.380
bias against group X.

00:32:57.381 --> 00:32:58.620
So we have X candidates and

00:32:58.621 --> 00:33:00.550
Y candidates depending on their group membership.

00:33:00.551 --> 00:33:02.695
And we're worried that whoever is making

00:33:02.696 --> 00:33:06.450
the decision has this bias against members of group X,

00:33:06.451 --> 00:33:08.980
where they sort of down weight them.

00:33:08.981 --> 00:33:11.440
And either it's implicit bias

00:33:11.441 --> 00:33:12.380
that they can't control or

00:33:12.381 --> 00:33:13.930
they somehow choose not to control.

00:33:13.931 --> 00:33:16.550
What do we do in those kinds of situations?

00:33:16.551 --> 00:33:19.130
So there's an intervention that's become increasingly

00:33:19.131 --> 00:33:22.235
popular in the business world,

00:33:22.236 --> 00:33:25.665
in academia, something called the Rooney Rule.

00:33:25.666 --> 00:33:26.840
And actually a number of you

00:33:26.841 --> 00:33:28.260
have no doubt encountered it,

00:33:28.261 --> 00:33:30.605
even if you haven't called it the Rooney Rule.

00:33:30.606 --> 00:33:34.940
And that's saying, when you're hiring for a job for say,

00:33:34.941 --> 00:33:37.525
a faculty position, an executive position, what have you.

00:33:37.526 --> 00:33:40.180
You commit that on your short list of interviewees,

00:33:40.181 --> 00:33:41.330
you promised that at least one of

00:33:41.331 --> 00:33:44.080
the people on that list will be from group X.

00:33:44.440 --> 00:33:47.020
&gt;&gt; This is the group

00:33:47.021 --> 00:33:48.370
you're concerned that you're discriminating against,

00:33:48.371 --> 00:33:49.575
so if you see a pool

00:33:49.576 --> 00:33:51.890
of interviewees and all of them are from

00:33:51.891 --> 00:33:53.800
group Y then whoever is higher up

00:33:53.801 --> 00:33:55.720
in management says, "I reject that list.

00:33:55.721 --> 00:33:57.870
You need to interview at least one person from group X."

00:33:57.871 --> 00:34:00.370
Why is this called the Rooney Rule?

00:34:00.371 --> 00:34:03.540
Because, the first really public pronouncement,

00:34:03.541 --> 00:34:04.455
and sort of commitment,

00:34:04.456 --> 00:34:06.600
so this actually came in 2002

00:34:06.601 --> 00:34:10.885
by the owners of the teams in National Football League.

00:34:10.886 --> 00:34:12.870
The Rooney Rule is named after Dan Rooney,

00:34:12.871 --> 00:34:14.975
who at the time was the owner of the Pittsburgh Steelers.

00:34:14.976 --> 00:34:18.040
Where looking at the incredibly low representation

00:34:18.041 --> 00:34:21.305
of minority head coaches in the NFL,

00:34:21.306 --> 00:34:23.500
which was of course especially striking given

00:34:23.501 --> 00:34:27.185
the high representation among the players for example.

00:34:27.186 --> 00:34:30.130
They want to figure out how can we increase

00:34:30.131 --> 00:34:31.690
the fraction of head coaches in the NFL

00:34:31.691 --> 00:34:33.555
who are from minority groups?

00:34:33.556 --> 00:34:38.010
And so this was the rule that they set out.

00:34:38.011 --> 00:34:39.610
And it's becoming increasingly popular a little

00:34:39.611 --> 00:34:41.400
because it somehow aims at

00:34:41.401 --> 00:34:45.370
the sort of middle layer between no intervention,

00:34:45.371 --> 00:34:47.015
just everyone should try harder,

00:34:47.016 --> 00:34:49.435
which felt like it somehow wasn't getting anywhere.

00:34:49.436 --> 00:34:51.140
And more heavyweight interventions,

00:34:51.141 --> 00:34:53.610
where for example you commit to actually hiring

00:34:53.611 --> 00:34:56.330
a certain number which then is subject to

00:34:56.331 --> 00:35:01.005
sort of significantly more complex territory

00:35:01.006 --> 00:35:03.730
in discrimination law.

00:35:03.731 --> 00:35:06.865
Right, so this is somehow something in the middle,

00:35:06.866 --> 00:35:08.420
you're committing to look extra hard

00:35:08.421 --> 00:35:10.485
at members of this group.

00:35:10.486 --> 00:35:12.495
So this has become

00:35:12.496 --> 00:35:14.830
again sort of increasing in popular intervention.

00:35:14.831 --> 00:35:19.040
It was invoked by Barack Obama in 2015,

00:35:19.041 --> 00:35:21.660
where he exhorted the tech industry to

00:35:21.661 --> 00:35:24.470
use this in executive hiring when a commission

00:35:24.471 --> 00:35:26.230
chaired by Eric Holder

00:35:26.231 --> 00:35:30.419
reviewed the situation of Uber in 2017,

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:31.810
they made this among their recommendations

00:35:31.811 --> 00:35:33.360
for executive hiring as well.

00:35:33.361 --> 00:35:37.570
Okay, that's the Rooney Rule.

00:35:37.571 --> 00:35:39.230
It had an effect actually,

00:35:39.231 --> 00:35:41.700
just to kind of show a little data here.

00:35:41.701 --> 00:35:42.685
This interesting paper by

00:35:42.686 --> 00:35:44.635
Cynthia DuBois from two years ago,

00:35:44.636 --> 00:35:46.725
looked at the moment the rule was introduced,

00:35:46.726 --> 00:35:48.460
just after the 2002 season.

00:35:48.461 --> 00:35:50.180
What happened to the representation

00:35:50.181 --> 00:35:52.265
of minority groups among NFL head coaches?

00:35:52.266 --> 00:35:53.790
And comparing it to a bunch of reference

00:35:53.791 --> 00:35:54.930
populations where the rule

00:35:54.931 --> 00:35:57.610
was not applied: offensive and defensive coordinators,

00:35:57.611 --> 00:35:59.520
head coaches of NCAA college teams,

00:35:59.521 --> 00:36:02.835
none of whom were actually using the Rooney Rule.

00:36:02.836 --> 00:36:03.950
Again there's a lot of noise,

00:36:03.951 --> 00:36:05.525
right, this is incredibly small numbers.

00:36:05.526 --> 00:36:07.590
But the two have been sort of roughly on top of

00:36:07.591 --> 00:36:09.695
each other and diverged sharply right on the field.

00:36:09.696 --> 00:36:11.645
So there's evidence that

00:36:11.646 --> 00:36:14.370
this was actually having an effect over time.

00:36:14.371 --> 00:36:18.910
But we actually want to ask in the spirit of this work of

00:36:18.911 --> 00:36:20.340
Scott Page who wants to think about

00:36:20.341 --> 00:36:23.780
the performance benefits of diversity, right.

00:36:23.781 --> 00:36:24.810
Independent of any of the

00:36:24.811 --> 00:36:26.100
other reasons that we might do it.

00:36:26.101 --> 00:36:28.280
Could it simply be a performance gain from

00:36:28.281 --> 00:36:31.830
imposing the Rooney Rule on people? Okay? Yes.

00:36:31.831 --> 00:36:34.520
&gt;&gt; Do the interviewers and interviewees

00:36:34.521 --> 00:36:35.840
know that this rule is being

00:36:35.841 --> 00:36:37.300
applied, when it is being applied?

00:36:37.301 --> 00:36:40.000
&gt;&gt; In the case of the NFL, yes.

00:36:40.001 --> 00:36:42.860
It's situation dependent,

00:36:42.861 --> 00:36:44.430
so the original articulation of it was,

00:36:44.431 --> 00:36:46.570
yes, there was an extremely public commitment

00:36:46.571 --> 00:36:49.115
to this and so everyone knew it was being applied.

00:36:49.116 --> 00:36:51.280
You can imagine situations where the firm

00:36:51.281 --> 00:36:53.390
decides to use it but not to announce it,

00:36:53.391 --> 00:36:55.065
and then you might have a different situation.

00:36:55.066 --> 00:36:57.090
&gt;&gt; Is there anything that happened that

00:36:57.091 --> 00:36:59.205
led to the dropping in the end?

00:36:59.206 --> 00:37:01.420
&gt;&gt; There's been some discussion that,

00:37:01.421 --> 00:37:03.340
there's sort of two theories about this.

00:37:03.341 --> 00:37:05.980
One is, it's small numbers, this is noise.

00:37:05.981 --> 00:37:08.355
The other could be

00:37:08.356 --> 00:37:09.890
there's concern that the rule is beginning to

00:37:09.891 --> 00:37:11.920
break down in that increasingly people have

00:37:11.921 --> 00:37:14.125
a sense of who they want as their head coach,

00:37:14.126 --> 00:37:16.210
and so you end up committing to

00:37:16.211 --> 00:37:18.440
somehow a set of interviews

00:37:18.441 --> 00:37:19.770
that don't actually have much power

00:37:19.771 --> 00:37:21.180
because you've sort of already decided.

00:37:21.181 --> 00:37:24.100
So there's concern that people have learned how to engage

00:37:24.101 --> 00:37:27.040
in the rules sort of in form,

00:37:27.041 --> 00:37:29.230
but not in spirit.

00:37:29.231 --> 00:37:32.515
It's unclear though, yes.

00:37:32.516 --> 00:37:36.000
Okay, so pure performance benefits from this.

00:37:36.001 --> 00:37:37.840
Why might there be a pure performance benefit

00:37:37.841 --> 00:37:38.760
from using the Rooney Rule in

00:37:38.761 --> 00:37:40.265
this case with probabilistic prediction?

00:37:40.266 --> 00:37:42.240
And again, for performance

00:37:42.241 --> 00:37:43.620
I need some kind of an objective function.

00:37:43.621 --> 00:37:44.360
I'm going to start with

00:37:44.361 --> 00:37:45.980
the objective function that just says,

00:37:45.981 --> 00:37:48.710
"I would like to maximize the sum of

00:37:48.711 --> 00:37:52.420
the qualities of the K people that I interview."

00:37:52.421 --> 00:37:54.490
There's a choice there because you could say, "Well,

00:37:54.491 --> 00:37:55.770
I'm just trying to hire one person,

00:37:55.771 --> 00:37:57.335
I should just maximize that person."

00:37:57.336 --> 00:37:59.420
But there's lots of evidence.

00:37:59.421 --> 00:38:00.630
One of the real motivations

00:38:00.631 --> 00:38:02.115
behind introducing rules like this,

00:38:02.116 --> 00:38:04.160
is that all K people you interview

00:38:04.161 --> 00:38:06.595
benefit from the exposure of being interviewed.

00:38:06.596 --> 00:38:08.980
For example, the people involved in interviewing them

00:38:08.981 --> 00:38:10.220
all go off to go work for

00:38:10.221 --> 00:38:11.550
different firms or different teams,

00:38:11.551 --> 00:38:13.610
or whatever over time, and

00:38:13.611 --> 00:38:14.750
they're going to be involved in recruiting,

00:38:14.751 --> 00:38:15.950
and they're going to have people who come to

00:38:15.951 --> 00:38:17.425
mind who are in their working memory,

00:38:17.426 --> 00:38:18.310
and if you were interviewed

00:38:18.311 --> 00:38:19.430
earlier there's a benefit to you.

00:38:19.431 --> 00:38:21.360
So I'll start with something where we're looking

00:38:21.361 --> 00:38:23.675
at some of the qualities of all k-people you interviewed,

00:38:23.676 --> 00:38:25.065
in that all of them are somehow,

00:38:25.066 --> 00:38:27.060
relevant to the situation.

00:38:27.061 --> 00:38:28.880
Here's a model where I'm going to try to

00:38:28.881 --> 00:38:30.010
suggest that there might be

00:38:30.011 --> 00:38:31.250
a performance benefit in

00:38:31.251 --> 00:38:32.755
cases where there's biased evaluation.

00:38:32.756 --> 00:38:34.270
The model is going to have

00:38:34.271 --> 00:38:36.420
the following three ingredients.

00:38:36.421 --> 00:38:39.560
First, I have a pool of job applicants.

00:38:39.561 --> 00:38:43.475
Each one has some potential for future performance.

00:38:43.476 --> 00:38:45.480
This can be a stylized model

00:38:45.481 --> 00:38:46.880
as with our previous set of slides,

00:38:46.881 --> 00:38:48.070
where I'm going to try to,

00:38:48.071 --> 00:38:49.630
what are sort of the minimal set of

00:38:49.631 --> 00:38:51.060
ingredients I'm going to need to be

00:38:51.061 --> 00:38:52.800
able to ask this question, okay?

00:38:52.801 --> 00:38:54.580
I'm going to need some kind of distribution

00:38:54.581 --> 00:38:56.255
over future performance, right.

00:38:56.256 --> 00:38:58.330
Hiring involves some uncertainty about like,

00:38:58.331 --> 00:39:01.410
who is it who's actually, who I'm going be getting?

00:39:01.411 --> 00:39:04.500
Most of the domains where the Rooney Rule is being

00:39:04.501 --> 00:39:07.820
applied are what you might call Creative professions,

00:39:07.821 --> 00:39:10.470
where there's a kind of unbounded domain

00:39:10.471 --> 00:39:12.270
of how good they can do in the future.

00:39:12.271 --> 00:39:14.015
Again, think about hiring

00:39:14.016 --> 00:39:16.920
researchers to take some crust numerical measure.

00:39:16.921 --> 00:39:18.390
Maybe we're trying to estimate at

00:39:18.391 --> 00:39:19.530
the end of their career what will

00:39:19.531 --> 00:39:22.240
be their Google Scholar citation count, right?

00:39:22.241 --> 00:39:24.735
A terrible thing to focus on as a unit, eventual quality,

00:39:24.736 --> 00:39:27.850
but sort of it conveys the idea, right?

00:39:27.851 --> 00:39:30.520
If we're using this rule to figure

00:39:30.521 --> 00:39:33.170
out who in our portfolio of recording artist we hired,

00:39:33.171 --> 00:39:34.940
we might be looking at song downloads,

00:39:34.941 --> 00:39:35.640
we might be looking at

00:39:35.641 --> 00:39:36.940
revenue generated if we're trying to

00:39:36.941 --> 00:39:39.395
hire sales people and so forth.

00:39:39.396 --> 00:39:41.420
All of these have the property that they

00:39:41.421 --> 00:39:43.330
tend to come from heavy-tailed distribution, right?

00:39:43.331 --> 00:39:44.920
There is an enormous amount of empirical work

00:39:44.921 --> 00:39:46.405
now and citation statistics,

00:39:46.406 --> 00:39:48.360
that say your lifetime

00:39:48.361 --> 00:39:50.815
future citations is as heavy-tailed distribution,

00:39:50.816 --> 00:39:53.230
in which outliers are quite common.

00:39:53.231 --> 00:39:55.110
So, imagine that your probability that

00:39:55.111 --> 00:39:56.950
your lifetime output exceeds T,

00:39:56.951 --> 00:39:59.285
is sum of T is the -1 + Delta.

00:39:59.286 --> 00:40:03.720
Okay? I think I actually have parenthesized that wrong,

00:40:03.721 --> 00:40:05.870
it should be the minus is outside the parentheses.

00:40:05.871 --> 00:40:09.290
Okay. So, good.

00:40:09.291 --> 00:40:11.470
So this is a domain in which this notion that

00:40:11.471 --> 00:40:13.710
we're trying to hire superstars,

00:40:13.711 --> 00:40:15.000
is somehow formalized by the fact that we

00:40:15.001 --> 00:40:16.550
have this very heavy-tailed distribution,

00:40:16.551 --> 00:40:18.590
you see really big numbers, that's important.

00:40:18.591 --> 00:40:20.040
Okay, second we have candidates

00:40:20.041 --> 00:40:21.390
applying from groups X and Y,

00:40:21.391 --> 00:40:23.480
and there are different compositions, right?

00:40:23.481 --> 00:40:25.155
If I have n candidates from group Y,

00:40:25.156 --> 00:40:26.720
the group's experiences no bias.

00:40:26.721 --> 00:40:29.830
I'll imagine I have Alpha times n, from group X.

00:40:29.831 --> 00:40:32.320
Okay. So, because the second thing

00:40:32.321 --> 00:40:33.660
that you have to worry about when you think about

00:40:33.661 --> 00:40:35.470
applying the Rooney Rule is how prevalent

00:40:35.471 --> 00:40:37.505
is this group in the applicant population?

00:40:37.506 --> 00:40:38.420
If there are only one percent

00:40:38.421 --> 00:40:39.470
of the applicant population,

00:40:39.471 --> 00:40:40.620
you pay a very large cost by

00:40:40.621 --> 00:40:42.165
committing one of your slots to them.

00:40:42.166 --> 00:40:43.730
If they're at parity,

00:40:43.731 --> 00:40:44.770
if Alpha equals one,

00:40:44.771 --> 00:40:46.880
then in fact the bias

00:40:46.881 --> 00:40:49.410
might be hurting a huge number of people.

00:40:49.411 --> 00:40:51.430
Okay, I'm going to make the assumption because

00:40:51.431 --> 00:40:53.525
it's sort of the most basic assumption you make that,

00:40:53.526 --> 00:40:56.720
all are drawn from the same power law distribution Z.

00:40:56.721 --> 00:40:58.650
We clearly look at the case where the two groups,

00:40:58.651 --> 00:41:01.810
maybe because of future bias they're going to experience

00:41:01.811 --> 00:41:03.535
different distributions going forward in the future,

00:41:03.536 --> 00:41:05.495
but we'll start with this one.

00:41:05.496 --> 00:41:08.420
And finally, so far there's no bias in this situation,

00:41:08.421 --> 00:41:10.365
so far groups X and Y are indistinguishable.

00:41:10.366 --> 00:41:11.925
Let's add bias.

00:41:11.926 --> 00:41:13.490
So there's some evidence

00:41:13.491 --> 00:41:16.560
from psychometric work on implicit bias,

00:41:16.561 --> 00:41:17.950
that for some of

00:41:17.951 --> 00:41:19.530
these estimates when you actually do experiments,

00:41:19.531 --> 00:41:20.910
have people look at CVs,

00:41:20.911 --> 00:41:22.700
make estimates relative to for example,

00:41:22.701 --> 00:41:24.205
true lifetime citation counts,

00:41:24.206 --> 00:41:26.790
that there's often something that looks

00:41:26.791 --> 00:41:29.325
like a multiplicative offset in these estimates.

00:41:29.326 --> 00:41:31.515
So imagine there's a bias parameter beta,

00:41:31.516 --> 00:41:33.090
Beta for bias, greater than one.

00:41:33.091 --> 00:41:35.830
So the selection committee picking people,

00:41:35.831 --> 00:41:38.915
believes that the potential of the IFX candidate,

00:41:38.916 --> 00:41:43.035
which is XI, they evaluate as XI divided by data.

00:41:43.036 --> 00:41:45.800
So their estimate is knocked down by data.

00:41:45.801 --> 00:41:47.920
That may again be because of implicit bias,

00:41:47.921 --> 00:41:49.240
they can't control may be because of explicit

00:41:49.241 --> 00:41:50.665
of bias that they are actually down rating it,

00:41:50.666 --> 00:41:52.335
but one way or another that's what's happening.

00:41:52.336 --> 00:41:56.195
And then they have to pick a shortlist of K. So,

00:41:56.196 --> 00:41:58.265
fundamentally it's this pipeline.

00:41:58.266 --> 00:41:59.820
Applicants are drawn from this,

00:41:59.821 --> 00:42:01.890
some are X candidates, some are Y candidates.

00:42:01.891 --> 00:42:03.520
They all get sorted,

00:42:03.521 --> 00:42:04.850
but the X candidates all get scaled

00:42:04.851 --> 00:42:06.030
down by this factor one over Beta,

00:42:06.031 --> 00:42:07.400
before they get merged sorted in.

00:42:07.401 --> 00:42:10.700
And then we pick the top K. So for example,

00:42:10.701 --> 00:42:12.010
here if K were three,

00:42:12.011 --> 00:42:14.120
the Rooney rule wouldn't be necessary because

00:42:14.121 --> 00:42:14.830
one of your three on

00:42:14.831 --> 00:42:16.670
your shortlist already is an X candidate.

00:42:16.671 --> 00:42:19.399
But if K were too, this list would be unacceptable,

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:23.355
you would need to pick this third candidate.

00:42:23.356 --> 00:42:25.850
And so here's the question that we ask,

00:42:25.851 --> 00:42:28.330
"Why might the Rooney Rule be helping?"

00:42:28.331 --> 00:42:30.635
After all I'm restricting your actions.

00:42:30.636 --> 00:42:32.980
Previously, you had an unconstrained optimization problem

00:42:32.981 --> 00:42:34.345
where you could pick any K you wanted.

00:42:34.346 --> 00:42:36.870
