Each has 40 percent of the people.

00:52:49.791 --> 00:52:51.510
Now, the best person in group

00:52:51.511 --> 00:52:53.260
A in expectation is better than the best person coming

00:52:53.261 --> 00:52:54.830
from group X because there are

00:52:54.831 --> 00:52:56.880
40 percent group A is only 20 percent

00:52:56.881 --> 00:52:58.685
[inaudible] The best person from group

00:52:58.686 --> 00:53:02.045
B is better than the best person group X.

00:53:02.046 --> 00:53:04.680
Yet for some reason,

00:53:04.681 --> 00:53:07.755
we're saying we're lumping together A and B.

00:53:07.756 --> 00:53:09.040
Each of whom is someone better than

00:53:09.041 --> 00:53:11.490
group X and we're treating group X separately.

00:53:11.491 --> 00:53:14.290
How can that possibly be improving utility, right?

00:53:14.291 --> 00:53:18.420
It seems wrong. Right?

00:53:18.421 --> 00:53:20.080
You see the problem.

00:53:20.081 --> 00:53:21.290
If I just chose to think

00:53:21.291 --> 00:53:22.810
of group X as having these two groups

00:53:22.811 --> 00:53:25.595
of 40 percent each. So what's going on?

00:53:25.596 --> 00:53:26.880
So, what's going on is

00:53:26.881 --> 00:53:30.010
that it's not the case that we're comparing

00:53:30.011 --> 00:53:32.800
the best in A

00:53:32.801 --> 00:53:34.050
and the best in X against the

00:53:34.051 --> 00:53:36.085
best in A and the best in B.

00:53:36.086 --> 00:53:39.495
In both cases, we're picking the best of A union B.

00:53:39.496 --> 00:53:41.120
We're always getting the better of those two.

00:53:41.121 --> 00:53:43.700
The other one we pick from

00:53:43.701 --> 00:53:47.555
A union B is known to be the second best.

00:53:47.556 --> 00:53:48.660
And when you have

00:53:48.661 --> 00:53:50.150
a very very heavy-tail power low whereas

00:53:50.151 --> 00:53:52.535
the person from group X because of the infinite bias,

00:53:52.536 --> 00:53:54.935
we have no idea what the upper bound on them is.

00:53:54.936 --> 00:53:56.845
And so the point is

00:53:56.846 --> 00:53:58.550
when you have a very heavy-tailed power law,

00:53:58.551 --> 00:54:00.840
the value of not having some conditional

00:54:00.841 --> 00:54:03.240
upper bound on their performance is extremely powerful.

00:54:03.241 --> 00:54:05.380
The fact that we know that the second white hand

00:54:05.381 --> 00:54:06.860
that we picked is

00:54:06.861 --> 00:54:08.710
not the top person already puts

00:54:08.711 --> 00:54:10.695
this big constraint on how high they- I mean,

00:54:10.696 --> 00:54:12.950
they're probably very good, but there's some upper bound.

00:54:12.951 --> 00:54:15.050
The person in group X has no upper bound because

00:54:15.051 --> 00:54:16.260
the bias may we never actually

00:54:16.261 --> 00:54:18.400
directly compare them to someone genuinely.

00:54:18.401 --> 00:54:21.470
Okay. So, that was the first thing I

00:54:21.471 --> 00:54:22.930
learned about bias that I really

00:54:22.931 --> 00:54:25.065
hadn't kind of understood.

00:54:25.066 --> 00:54:27.110
But it really follows from

00:54:27.111 --> 00:54:28.350
very simple assumptions and it's interesting

00:54:28.351 --> 00:54:29.825
it's happening in this power law regime,

00:54:29.826 --> 00:54:34.175
which is exactly the realm of Google Scholar counts,

00:54:34.176 --> 00:54:36.290
view counts, download statistics where we

00:54:36.291 --> 00:54:38.990
often apply things like the Rooney Rule.

00:54:38.991 --> 00:54:41.590
Let me tell you the second thing I learned

00:54:41.591 --> 00:54:44.395
about bias from this.

00:54:44.396 --> 00:54:46.960
And that's the following,

00:54:46.961 --> 00:54:49.715
so when we go to analyze this,

00:54:49.716 --> 00:54:52.580
the basic object that we need to understand in

00:54:52.581 --> 00:54:54.140
this analysis is what you might call an

00:54:54.141 --> 00:54:56.655
Expectation Conditional on Comparison.

00:54:56.656 --> 00:54:58.810
Okay, so that's the following thing.

00:54:58.811 --> 00:55:00.700
The expectation of some random variable A,

00:55:00.701 --> 00:55:02.380
given that A is greater

00:55:02.381 --> 00:55:04.310
than constant C times some other random variable B.

00:55:04.311 --> 00:55:06.060
Think of A and B as sort of the top

00:55:06.061 --> 00:55:07.575
that is kind of a stylized thing,

00:55:07.576 --> 00:55:09.335
abstracting from the analysis we're doing.

00:55:09.336 --> 00:55:11.680
Think of A and B as the top candidates in the two groups.

00:55:11.681 --> 00:55:12.820
C is some level of bias.

00:55:12.821 --> 00:55:16.490
It says, "When we pick A,

00:55:16.491 --> 00:55:17.760
it's because they have to be like bigger

00:55:17.761 --> 00:55:19.175
than two times B."

00:55:19.176 --> 00:55:20.190
And so we're interested in

00:55:20.191 --> 00:55:21.090
how they are given that they're

00:55:21.091 --> 00:55:22.640
bigger than two times the top candidate

00:55:22.641 --> 00:55:23.050
in the other group,

00:55:23.051 --> 00:55:24.710
because there's a bias to a factor of two, right.

00:55:24.711 --> 00:55:26.310
So now we're back in the world of

00:55:26.311 --> 00:55:29.250
finite bias where things are more complicated.

00:55:29.890 --> 00:55:32.370
And so, syntactically it's

00:55:32.371 --> 00:55:33.670
very compact thing to write down.

00:55:33.671 --> 00:55:36.320
We felt like we understood it going in but it turns

00:55:36.321 --> 00:55:40.380
out this kind of construction is a real mess, okay.

00:55:40.381 --> 00:55:42.130
This idea that this person

00:55:42.131 --> 00:55:43.850
is better than twice this other person,

00:55:43.851 --> 00:55:45.690
both of whom were drawn from some distribution.

00:55:45.691 --> 00:55:49.520
So, and this actually showed up in the empirical work.

00:55:49.521 --> 00:55:50.780
For example, in the Cynthia DuBois's work

00:55:50.781 --> 00:55:52.080
and the Rooney Rule,

00:55:52.081 --> 00:55:53.825
where an actual question to ask is,

00:55:53.826 --> 00:55:55.430
"What was the win-loss record

00:55:55.431 --> 00:55:56.750
of minority head coaches before

00:55:56.751 --> 00:55:58.000
the Rooney Rule and

00:55:58.001 --> 00:55:59.490
what was the win-loss record afterwards?"

00:55:59.491 --> 00:56:01.035
And you actually find again,

00:56:01.036 --> 00:56:02.240
there are small numbers and now there's

00:56:02.241 --> 00:56:03.280
really small numbers being involved.

00:56:03.281 --> 00:56:08.940
But that somehow towards some approximation,

00:56:08.941 --> 00:56:10.500
the win-loss record minority head coaches before

00:56:10.501 --> 00:56:12.640
the Rooney Rule was actually slightly higher,

00:56:12.641 --> 00:56:14.010
potentially because they were passing through

00:56:14.011 --> 00:56:15.640
a stronger filter because of the bias.

00:56:15.641 --> 00:56:19.445
Afterwards they became more equalized.

00:56:19.446 --> 00:56:20.435
That's sort of intuitive,

00:56:20.436 --> 00:56:21.955
higher filters should mean they're doing better.

00:56:21.956 --> 00:56:23.890
But is that really what's going on?

00:56:23.891 --> 00:56:24.890
So let's say I have

00:56:24.891 --> 00:56:26.030
these independent random variables A and

00:56:26.031 --> 00:56:28.210
B drawn from some distribution and I'm going

00:56:28.211 --> 00:56:30.360
to find a function of C. I'm going to sweep over

00:56:30.361 --> 00:56:34.055
the bias and ask what happens to this expectation.

00:56:34.056 --> 00:56:36.760
In other words, how good is a candidate

00:56:36.761 --> 00:56:38.155
A experiencing bias when they have to be

00:56:38.156 --> 00:56:40.000
twice as good as the competition because of bias?

00:56:40.001 --> 00:56:42.020
How could your expectancy be when they have to be

00:56:42.021 --> 00:56:44.420
three times as good as the competition because of bias?

00:56:44.421 --> 00:56:46.480
How about 10 times as good? Alright, that's what

00:56:46.481 --> 00:56:48.820
happens as I sweep across C. So F

00:56:48.821 --> 00:56:50.890
of C is how good I expect this candidate to be when

00:56:50.891 --> 00:56:53.855
they're subjected to a level bias equal to a factor of C?

00:56:53.856 --> 00:56:56.225
What can we say about this?

00:56:56.226 --> 00:56:57.020
For example,

00:56:57.021 --> 00:56:58.695
here's the most simple thing you'd like to ask,

00:56:58.696 --> 00:57:00.655
is it monotone increasing in C?

00:57:00.656 --> 00:57:01.930
If candidates have to pass through

00:57:01.931 --> 00:57:03.310
increasing levels of bias,

00:57:03.311 --> 00:57:06.280
does it follow an expectation their better?

00:57:06.281 --> 00:57:08.600
Okay. I could tell this actually

00:57:08.601 --> 00:57:10.925
as say a parable about hiring.

00:57:10.926 --> 00:57:14.165
Let's say I had two research labs.

00:57:14.166 --> 00:57:15.870
Both research labs prefer

00:57:15.871 --> 00:57:17.530
hiring applied people to theory people.

00:57:17.531 --> 00:57:20.370
So in the first research lab, every year,

00:57:20.371 --> 00:57:21.450
there going to interview a

00:57:21.451 --> 00:57:23.170
theory candidate and an applied candidate.

00:57:23.171 --> 00:57:24.600
And the theory candidate has to be

00:57:24.601 --> 00:57:25.810
at least twice as good as

00:57:25.811 --> 00:57:28.545
the applied candidate to get hired.

00:57:28.546 --> 00:57:30.825
The other lab drawing from the same distribution,

00:57:30.826 --> 00:57:34.165
interviews a theory candidate and applied candidate.

00:57:34.166 --> 00:57:36.180
But the theory candidate has be three times as good

00:57:36.181 --> 00:57:37.350
or more compared to

00:57:37.351 --> 00:57:38.460
the applied candidate in order to get hired.

00:57:38.461 --> 00:57:39.830
Now we're going to look back

00:57:39.831 --> 00:57:41.735
retrospectively 20 years later,

00:57:41.736 --> 00:57:44.695
there will be fewer theory candidates at the second lab.

00:57:44.696 --> 00:57:46.160
Will they be better on average

00:57:46.161 --> 00:57:48.045
because they have to pass through the higher filter?

00:57:48.046 --> 00:57:50.120
That's the monotonous question.

00:57:50.121 --> 00:57:52.215
The answer is not necessarily.

00:57:52.216 --> 00:57:54.800
For some distributions, they might actually be lower.

00:57:54.801 --> 00:57:56.290
In that's sends to which I mean

00:57:56.291 --> 00:57:58.180
this quality is a complete mess

00:57:58.181 --> 00:57:59.775
that we don't really fully understand.

00:57:59.776 --> 00:58:02.965
Here's a simple example to convince you of this.

00:58:02.966 --> 00:58:04.565
Imagine this is the distribution.

00:58:04.566 --> 00:58:05.825
So it's just a four-point distribution.

00:58:05.826 --> 00:58:06.250
Actually there are

00:58:06.251 --> 00:58:08.265
even simpler distributions where this happens.

00:58:08.266 --> 00:58:10.865
So imagine, every year this

00:58:10.866 --> 00:58:12.060
theory an applied candidate drawn

00:58:12.061 --> 00:58:13.400
from distribution where the quantity is one,

00:58:13.401 --> 00:58:15.795
five, nine or 13 uniformly random.

00:58:15.796 --> 00:58:18.200
So the sample space is just this four by four grid,

00:58:18.201 --> 00:58:19.740
there are 16 possible kinds

00:58:19.741 --> 00:58:21.360
of recruiting years that you can have.

00:58:21.361 --> 00:58:22.900
Right? This is the quality of the theory candidate,

00:58:22.901 --> 00:58:24.310
this is the quality of the applied candidate.

00:58:24.311 --> 00:58:26.760
What does the event A greater than 2B look like?

00:58:26.761 --> 00:58:29.420
It says in these four kinds of years,

00:58:29.421 --> 00:58:31.390
you end up hiring the theory candidate.

00:58:31.391 --> 00:58:33.370
So, when the theory candidate is 13 and

00:58:33.371 --> 00:58:34.430
the applied candidate is five

00:58:34.431 --> 00:58:35.720
that's greater than two times five.

00:58:35.721 --> 00:58:37.690
So you hire them. Similarly with these three.

00:58:37.691 --> 00:58:39.260
And so your expected value was

00:58:39.261 --> 00:58:41.545
the average quality over these which is 10.

00:58:41.546 --> 00:58:43.845
We have to be three times as good,

00:58:43.846 --> 00:58:45.505
then the slope shift downward

00:58:45.506 --> 00:58:48.570
and you're only averaging over these three points.

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:51.480
Because you can only hire a theory candidate in

00:58:51.481 --> 00:58:53.940
this world when the applied candidate was really,

00:58:53.941 --> 00:58:55.170
really bad, which actually

00:58:55.171 --> 00:58:56.635
puts less of a constraint with you.

00:58:56.636 --> 00:58:59.565
So here actually you do the average, and it's nine.

00:58:59.566 --> 00:59:01.860
So it says, if I would go and look at the research labs

00:59:01.861 --> 00:59:04.810
20 years later in this very stylize scenario,

00:59:04.811 --> 00:59:07.790
the research lab with the higher filter actually

00:59:07.791 --> 00:59:11.480
has worse theory candidates people on average.

00:59:11.481 --> 00:59:13.440
And in real life,

00:59:13.441 --> 00:59:14.770
we would look at this and we'd say that could be for

00:59:14.771 --> 00:59:16.480
any number of reasons which in real life would

00:59:16.481 --> 00:59:20.210
probably be the dominant reasons for example a lab with

00:59:20.211 --> 00:59:22.510
that high a filter is probably going to be unwelcoming to

00:59:22.511 --> 00:59:23.870
theoreticians who won't want to go there

00:59:23.871 --> 00:59:25.610
and so that's why you have weaker people there.

00:59:25.611 --> 00:59:27.670
Or if they really have such a high filter,

00:59:27.671 --> 00:59:29.090
they're probably also bad at evaluating

00:59:29.091 --> 00:59:30.635
theoreticians as they pick the wrong people.

00:59:30.636 --> 00:59:31.625
Maybe that's true also.

00:59:31.626 --> 00:59:33.270
But neither of those has to be true.

00:59:33.271 --> 00:59:35.475
This could happen for purely arithmetical reasons.

00:59:35.476 --> 00:59:38.060
And that's somehow the point that this is

00:59:38.061 --> 00:59:40.680
a very messy concept that we're dealing with.

00:59:40.681 --> 00:59:42.130
And somehow this is at the heart of what we

00:59:42.131 --> 00:59:43.670
have to do when we analyze the effect of

00:59:43.671 --> 00:59:45.520
something like the Rooney Rule we're looking at

00:59:45.521 --> 00:59:47.490
these expectations conditional on

00:59:47.491 --> 00:59:50.075
comparison. Yeah, question.

00:59:50.076 --> 00:59:52.090
&gt;&gt; Would this example hold

00:59:52.091 --> 00:59:54.760
up if DNB coming from heavy tail distributions.

00:59:54.761 --> 00:59:57.270
&gt;&gt; No. Actually for heavy tail distributions,

00:59:57.271 --> 00:59:58.730
one thing we have to prove which

00:59:58.731 --> 01:00:00.485
this suggests but not really

01:00:00.486 --> 01:00:04.845
at least for power law distributions,

01:00:04.846 --> 01:00:06.370
that function is monotone, which is somehow

01:00:06.371 --> 01:00:08.290
the first step we need.

01:00:08.291 --> 01:00:10.420
You could simply have a continuous density function

01:00:10.421 --> 01:00:12.120
where it's not monotone.

01:00:12.121 --> 01:00:13.360
I just put some bumps into it

