01:03:09.871 --> 01:03:11.260
So fundamentally what we have been

01:03:11.261 --> 01:03:12.500
talking about is an event where we

01:03:12.501 --> 01:03:15.325
are choosing a short list of K people out of N people.

01:03:15.326 --> 01:03:16.470
And there have really been

01:03:16.471 --> 01:03:17.525
two things we have been talking about.

01:03:17.526 --> 01:03:19.965
One is how do we evaluate individuals?

01:03:19.966 --> 01:03:22.260
What score do we give them? And the other

01:03:22.261 --> 01:03:24.210
is this question of how do we evaluate the set?

01:03:24.211 --> 01:03:25.520
And so far the set we have just been summing.

01:03:25.521 --> 01:03:27.060
But we might want to use

01:03:27.061 --> 01:03:29.805
some other function to evaluate the set.

01:03:29.806 --> 01:03:32.985
And what happens if we have some other function?

01:03:32.986 --> 01:03:34.300
Right? So the point is if we are

01:03:34.301 --> 01:03:35.760
just evaluating by the sum,

01:03:35.761 --> 01:03:36.870
we should be doing what we have been doing.

01:03:36.871 --> 01:03:38.720
We should compute everybody's expected value

01:03:38.721 --> 01:03:40.310
and take the Max expected value.

01:03:40.311 --> 01:03:41.530
But what if we are back in

01:03:41.531 --> 01:03:42.700
this interviewing context where

01:03:42.701 --> 01:03:43.440
really the reason we look to

01:03:43.441 --> 01:03:45.295
K people is to pick the best one?

01:03:45.296 --> 01:03:47.580
Should we actually, right, okay.

01:03:47.581 --> 01:03:48.890
So imagine the following set

01:03:48.891 --> 01:03:50.280
up. I look at the applicants.

01:03:50.281 --> 01:03:52.030
I have to assign each one a numerical score.

01:03:52.031 --> 01:03:53.910
I have to rank them by their score.

01:03:53.911 --> 01:03:55.985
But am going to be judged on

01:03:55.986 --> 01:03:57.315
the best person I pick

01:03:57.316 --> 01:03:59.275
because maybe this is an interview context,

01:03:59.276 --> 01:04:00.620
maybe I am a graduate

01:04:00.621 --> 01:04:02.020
admissions committee that says what we really

01:04:02.021 --> 01:04:03.600
want is for the most famous graduate

01:04:03.601 --> 01:04:05.085
from our program to be as high as possible,

01:04:05.086 --> 01:04:07.030
maybe we are choosing a team or we want,

01:04:07.031 --> 01:04:08.420
we are going to be judged by the best

01:04:08.421 --> 01:04:09.850
idea anyone the team has.

01:04:09.851 --> 01:04:11.720
But for all those reasons Max

01:04:11.721 --> 01:04:14.040
is kind of a natural thing to look at.

01:04:14.041 --> 01:04:17.000
How do we do if we use the expectation as a score?

01:04:17.001 --> 01:04:18.540
Is there some other score we should be using?

01:04:18.541 --> 01:04:21.020
So let me show you one final example,

01:04:21.021 --> 01:04:22.520
which I think also sort of teaches us something

01:04:22.521 --> 01:04:24.725
about the problem with assigning scores.

01:04:24.726 --> 01:04:26.960
So this is the sort of, you can call, contest objective.

01:04:26.961 --> 01:04:28.710
If we can bring in the case shortlist we are going to

01:04:28.711 --> 01:04:30.800
pick one after the interview.

01:04:30.801 --> 01:04:32.815
They were scored by the Max.

01:04:32.816 --> 01:04:35.960
So using the mean to sort

01:04:35.961 --> 01:04:40.255
people has the following worrisome behavior.

01:04:40.256 --> 01:04:42.265
Think of K as large here. Suppose I just had a pool.

01:04:42.266 --> 01:04:43.790
I had two K applicants.

01:04:43.791 --> 01:04:46.190
K of them produce high risk high reward.

01:04:46.191 --> 01:04:46.890
They produce value K

01:04:46.891 --> 01:04:47.910
minus one of them is probably one over

01:04:47.911 --> 01:04:51.760
K. And K of them are really safe deterministic.

01:04:51.761 --> 01:04:54.110
They produce value one with probably one.

01:04:54.111 --> 01:04:56.240
So a test to evaluate

01:04:56.241 --> 01:04:58.280
expectation chooses the second group of candidates

01:04:58.281 --> 01:04:59.700
because they are expedition one

01:04:59.701 --> 01:05:02.180
rather than one minus a tiny bit.

01:05:02.181 --> 01:05:05.665
But as you might not be surprised to hear,

01:05:05.666 --> 01:05:07.295
that would be a bad choice.

01:05:07.296 --> 01:05:08.940
I should really do is,

01:05:08.941 --> 01:05:10.315
I would truly do much better by picking

01:05:10.316 --> 01:05:13.165
the K high risk high reward ones.

01:05:13.166 --> 01:05:17.330
Why? Because each of them succeeds overwhelmingly in

01:05:17.331 --> 01:05:18.760
this case with probably one over

01:05:18.761 --> 01:05:19.950
K. So the chance that

01:05:19.951 --> 01:05:22.040
at least one of them succeeds is a constant.

01:05:22.041 --> 01:05:25.050
It's one minus one minus one over K to the K,

01:05:25.051 --> 01:05:27.660
which is converging to one minus over E. So it

01:05:27.661 --> 01:05:30.580
is constant probability I read the enormous reward.

01:05:30.581 --> 01:05:32.520
And so an expectation and again we're

01:05:32.521 --> 01:05:34.815
looking at the expected value I am doing much better.

01:05:34.816 --> 01:05:39.350
So ranking by expected value is hurting me a lot

01:05:39.351 --> 01:05:40.540
if my goal is to maximize

01:05:40.541 --> 01:05:43.605
the expected quality of the Max.

01:05:43.606 --> 01:05:46.840
Now Scott Page came across

01:05:46.841 --> 01:05:50.650
similar examples and sort of ended up there and said,

01:05:50.651 --> 01:05:52.220
this is the problem with using scores.

01:05:52.221 --> 01:05:54.270
Right, when you score people and you rank them, you know,

01:05:54.271 --> 01:05:56.820
he uses this as an indictment of SAT's and GRE's that

01:05:56.821 --> 01:05:59.390
is reducing people to a uni-dimensional number,

01:05:59.391 --> 01:06:01.250
and then when you sort and rank you might

01:06:01.251 --> 01:06:03.490
actually miss the opportunity to do really well.

01:06:03.491 --> 01:06:05.970
So, my thrill Ragav and I

01:06:05.971 --> 01:06:07.210
looked at this and we

01:06:07.211 --> 01:06:08.490
are intrigued by the following question.

01:06:08.491 --> 01:06:10.060
Is the problem with scores or is

01:06:10.061 --> 01:06:11.805
the problem with expected value as the score?

01:06:11.806 --> 01:06:13.665
Just because the expected value is not working,

01:06:13.666 --> 01:06:16.890
maybe there is some more clever uni-dimensional score

01:06:16.891 --> 01:06:19.240
I could be using that would actually let me do well.

01:06:19.241 --> 01:06:21.845
And the answer is that that's actually what is happening.

01:06:21.846 --> 01:06:23.680
Here is a better test that you could

01:06:23.681 --> 01:06:25.325
use that I am trying to pick the Max.

01:06:25.326 --> 01:06:27.130
Rather than say let us look at

01:06:27.131 --> 01:06:29.140
the mean performance of candidate I,

01:06:29.141 --> 01:06:31.520
let us look at a different quantity.

01:06:31.521 --> 01:06:32.890
Let us take candidate I,

01:06:32.891 --> 01:06:34.130
and again we are in a stylized role

01:06:34.131 --> 01:06:35.420
where we assume we know their distribution.

01:06:35.421 --> 01:06:37.400
Let us imagine you drew

01:06:37.401 --> 01:06:38.490
K times from

01:06:38.491 --> 01:06:41.035
that person's distribution and then you took the Max.

01:06:41.036 --> 01:06:43.460
So this is the best they would do if they

01:06:43.461 --> 01:06:45.735
live their life K times over independently.

01:06:45.736 --> 01:06:47.220
I mean, this is their upside potential.

01:06:47.221 --> 01:06:48.720
Let me use that as the score.

01:06:48.721 --> 01:06:50.705
It is again just the single number.

01:06:50.706 --> 01:06:52.330
So I throw away all the information

01:06:52.331 --> 01:06:54.220
about the distribution. I just keep that one number.

01:06:54.221 --> 01:06:57.310
That is now my new GRE score or my new SAT score.

01:06:57.311 --> 01:06:58.970
And I sort by that.

01:06:58.971 --> 01:07:00.745
Then the theorem you can prove

01:07:00.746 --> 01:07:03.095
is that there is some absolute constants,

01:07:03.096 --> 01:07:04.830
C. So this is actually a factor

01:07:04.831 --> 01:07:07.000
of C approximation to the optimum.

01:07:07.001 --> 01:07:09.080
Right? If I could know everything about

01:07:09.081 --> 01:07:10.920
the distribution and solve

01:07:10.921 --> 01:07:12.650
the NP Hard Optimization Problem of picking

01:07:12.651 --> 01:07:13.600
the K that we are going to have

01:07:13.601 --> 01:07:15.290
the highest expected maximum,

01:07:15.291 --> 01:07:17.190
I would not do better

01:07:17.191 --> 01:07:18.890
than a factor of C in terms of what I

01:07:18.891 --> 01:07:20.030
am doing by simply computing this

01:07:20.031 --> 01:07:22.745
metric and sorting on it.

01:07:22.746 --> 01:07:24.790
So there are a couple of things,

01:07:24.791 --> 01:07:26.840
and this will be my final point here.

01:07:26.841 --> 01:07:29.470
A couple of things that we learn from this, right?

01:07:29.471 --> 01:07:34.860
One is that just because a vanilla test is not working,

01:07:34.861 --> 01:07:36.680
like the expected value, does not mean

01:07:36.681 --> 01:07:38.660
that the idea of uni-dimensional test is not working.

01:07:38.661 --> 01:07:40.830
Sometimes you just need a more subtle test.

01:07:40.831 --> 01:07:42.280
The other is that it was sort of striking

01:07:42.281 --> 01:07:44.650
that you know we often think about

01:07:44.651 --> 01:07:46.750
the importance of hiring

01:07:46.751 --> 01:07:48.810
employees or admitting students based on potential.

01:07:48.811 --> 01:07:50.420
Right? We are not looking at performance.

01:07:50.421 --> 01:07:52.510
We are looking at potential. The question

01:07:52.511 --> 01:07:54.115
is what might potential mean?

01:07:54.116 --> 01:07:56.010
This is sort of one argument that it is saying

01:07:56.011 --> 01:07:57.890
let us not look at their mean value,

01:07:57.891 --> 01:07:59.220
let us look at their sort of top one

01:07:59.221 --> 01:08:00.910
over K quantile value.

01:08:00.911 --> 01:08:02.330
That's their upside potential.

01:08:02.331 --> 01:08:04.000
And notice that K is scaled

01:08:04.001 --> 01:08:05.730
to the size of the team that you are picking.

01:08:05.731 --> 01:08:08.130
So if I am admitting 10 people I

01:08:08.131 --> 01:08:10.840
am interested in the one tenth quantile.

01:08:10.841 --> 01:08:12.250
If I'm admitting 100 people

01:08:12.251 --> 01:08:13.865
I might be interested in [inaudible] quantile.

01:08:13.866 --> 01:08:16.295
Again, in a world where we are optimizing the Max.

01:08:16.296 --> 01:08:19.360
The Max as a particular thing you might want to optimize.

01:08:19.361 --> 01:08:20.610
More generally, there is

01:08:20.611 --> 01:08:21.880
a lot of interesting questions here,

01:08:21.881 --> 01:08:23.710
some of which we have resolved some of which we have not.

01:08:23.711 --> 01:08:25.065
So many questions about

01:08:25.066 --> 01:08:27.190
given the way in which you evaluate sets,

01:08:27.191 --> 01:08:28.920
is there a single uni-dimensional

01:08:28.921 --> 01:08:29.930
test score you can apply?

01:08:29.931 --> 01:08:31.615
And the answer is sometimes yes sometimes no.

01:08:31.616 --> 01:08:33.510
You can construct situations where

01:08:33.511 --> 01:08:35.650
there is no single number you can put on

01:08:35.651 --> 01:08:36.740
each person's distribution that

01:08:36.741 --> 01:08:37.550
actually gets you to within

01:08:37.551 --> 01:08:39.810
a constant factor. All right.

01:08:39.811 --> 01:08:43.230
So with that all wrapped up,

01:08:43.231 --> 01:08:46.225
as I said this has been in part, a definitional activity.

01:08:46.226 --> 01:08:49.710
Right? Going into a lot of the literature on fairness,

01:08:49.711 --> 01:08:52.250
on diversity and selection, on bias selection.

01:08:52.251 --> 01:08:56.120
And to think about some of the basic rules that

01:08:56.121 --> 01:08:58.310
people are using trying to build models around them so

01:08:58.311 --> 01:09:00.665
we can get some insights into what is going on.

01:09:00.666 --> 01:09:01.820
In all these cases, I think,

01:09:01.821 --> 01:09:03.045
what we are finding is you can get

01:09:03.046 --> 01:09:04.410
quite complex behaviors from

01:09:04.411 --> 01:09:07.210
relatively simple interventions.

01:09:07.211 --> 01:09:09.070
Right? Things like the Rooney Rule,

01:09:09.071 --> 01:09:12.285
things like selecting people so as to achieve the Max.

01:09:12.286 --> 01:09:13.270
You can write in

01:09:13.271 --> 01:09:14.820
very simple specifications that induce very,

01:09:14.821 --> 01:09:17.965
very complicated behaviors.

01:09:17.966 --> 01:09:20.235
And I think all of these lead to

01:09:20.236 --> 01:09:22.150
interesting open questions when we

01:09:22.151 --> 01:09:24.110
embed this into some kind of larger context.

01:09:24.111 --> 01:09:25.645
In the end, the reason we introduce

01:09:25.646 --> 01:09:27.250
an intervention like re-scores,

01:09:27.251 --> 01:09:28.950
the reason we introduce an intervention

01:09:28.951 --> 01:09:30.700
like the Rooney Rule,

01:09:30.701 --> 01:09:32.175
is because we are thinking about

01:09:32.176 --> 01:09:34.170
the dynamics of time going forward.

01:09:34.171 --> 01:09:35.190
We are thinking about what the world is

01:09:35.191 --> 01:09:36.260
going to look like five years

01:09:36.261 --> 01:09:38.710
from now or 10 years from now if we implement this rule.

01:09:38.711 --> 01:09:40.680
And, of course, dynamics over time are going

01:09:40.681 --> 01:09:42.555
to introduce their own set of complex behaviors.

01:09:42.556 --> 01:09:44.260
They are going to change the mix of people that we

01:09:44.261 --> 01:09:47.415
see in the training and test sets that we work with.

01:09:47.416 --> 01:09:49.880
They actually even, as the economists tell us,

01:09:49.881 --> 01:09:52.375
change people's decisions about effort.

01:09:52.376 --> 01:09:54.960
Because if you are being evaluated in

01:09:54.961 --> 01:09:56.760
a biased world and there is bias

01:09:56.761 --> 01:09:59.350
against you there is essentially a tax on your effort.

01:09:59.351 --> 01:10:01.425
For every unit of effort you put in,

01:10:01.426 --> 01:10:02.990
you are only getting back one minus

01:10:02.991 --> 01:10:04.490
Epsilon reward because some of

01:10:04.491 --> 01:10:05.970
that effort is actually being

01:10:05.971 --> 01:10:07.610
lost to the bias against you.

01:10:07.611 --> 01:10:09.520
And in that kind of a world,

01:10:09.521 --> 01:10:11.400
people where this bias against are actually make

01:10:11.401 --> 01:10:13.195
different decisions about effort.

01:10:13.196 --> 01:10:14.500
They are actually going to

01:10:14.501 --> 01:10:16.575
rationally potentially invest less effort.

01:10:16.576 --> 01:10:19.330
Thereby, you know, sort of in

01:10:19.331 --> 01:10:22.140
an unfortunate way, reinforcing the bias.

01:10:22.141 --> 01:10:23.580
Right? Because of the bias,

01:10:23.581 --> 01:10:25.160
they invest less effort and

01:10:25.161 --> 01:10:26.780
that somehow confirms what people believe in

01:10:26.781 --> 01:10:28.240
the bias and you get into this very

01:10:28.241 --> 01:10:30.270
dangerous positive feedback loop.

01:10:30.271 --> 01:10:32.040
The point being some of these interventions

01:10:32.041 --> 01:10:33.840
can help break that feedback loop.

01:10:33.841 --> 01:10:35.310
If we force people to look beyond

01:10:35.311 --> 01:10:37.100
the bias they have that actually changes

01:10:37.101 --> 01:10:40.695
the incentive calculation the people in the biased group

01:10:40.696 --> 01:10:42.840
might make about investing effort and

01:10:42.841 --> 01:10:45.190
actually break out of that.

01:10:45.191 --> 01:10:46.770
So I think there are some very interesting questions

01:10:46.771 --> 01:10:48.300
about the dynamics over time

01:10:48.301 --> 01:10:49.570
both in the data that we see in

01:10:49.571 --> 01:10:51.270
the incentives and that is going to,

01:10:51.271 --> 01:10:52.390
I think, lead to a lot of interesting work

01:10:52.391 --> 01:10:54.780
going forward. Thanks very much.

01:11:02.240 --> 01:11:05.310
&gt;&gt; So, if I brought a,

01:11:05.311 --> 01:11:08.750
especially given the challenges with

01:11:08.751 --> 01:11:12.320
the [inaudible] folks and sensitivity analysis applied to this.

01:11:12.321 --> 01:11:14.050
Do you have a sense for

01:11:14.051 --> 01:11:17.760
various kinds of variants over what we you know really

01:11:17.761 --> 01:11:19.970
slamming the results in

01:11:20.340 --> 01:11:24.060
doing the job on them in terms of the clarity.

01:11:24.061 --> 01:11:26.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So I think there is a.

01:11:26.751 --> 01:11:28.300
&gt;&gt; If they have a question you might [inaudible].

01:11:28.301 --> 01:11:30.560
&gt;&gt; The question is given

01:11:30.561 --> 01:11:32.160
the difficulties in forming

01:11:32.161 --> 01:11:33.590
accurate evaluations of people,

01:11:33.591 --> 01:11:36.040
what happens when there is variance in the estimates?

01:11:36.041 --> 01:11:38.140
Yeah, I guess I would say two things,

01:11:38.141 --> 01:11:41.255
which is, notion variances operating at two levels.

01:11:41.256 --> 01:11:42.790
First of all, all of

01:11:42.791 --> 01:11:45.150
these cases we are talking with people

01:11:45.151 --> 01:11:46.360
where what I see about them is

01:11:46.361 --> 01:11:47.620
a distribution and

01:11:47.621 --> 01:11:49.160
their true performance can be drawn from that definition.

01:11:49.161 --> 01:11:51.480
So in some sense the models all build variants in.

01:11:51.481 --> 01:11:53.090
And I think what is telling us is that the

01:11:53.091 --> 01:11:54.720
heavier the tail and the power law

01:11:54.721 --> 01:11:56.450
the more uncertainty I have about how

01:11:56.451 --> 01:11:58.290
large the outlier is going to be.

01:11:58.291 --> 01:12:00.450
So in a way these all sort of

01:12:00.451 --> 01:12:02.960
almost start from the difficulty in forming...

01:12:02.961 --> 01:12:03.400
Right,

01:12:03.401 --> 01:12:04.870
all of these questions what would be different if I had

01:12:04.871 --> 01:12:06.290
a deterministic evaluation of

01:12:06.291 --> 01:12:07.795
the person being drawn description.

01:12:07.796 --> 01:12:09.740
But then there is error in the preamble as.

01:12:09.741 --> 01:12:11.230
Error in the parameters now is

01:12:11.231 --> 01:12:13.060
sort of the second level of this.

01:12:13.061 --> 01:12:17.440
Yeah. So I think again in some of

01:12:17.441 --> 01:12:18.950
these cases I think what is happening

01:12:18.951 --> 01:12:20.850
is it gets much

01:12:20.851 --> 01:12:22.440
more challenging when there is large variance.

01:12:22.441 --> 01:12:24.680
So most of the results that we have

01:12:24.681 --> 01:12:27.330
basically say for sufficiently small errors,

01:12:27.331 --> 01:12:30.320
we get roughly something that sort of changes

01:12:30.321 --> 01:12:31.830
continuously and what I have been talking about here.

01:12:31.831 --> 01:12:33.660
But it is an interesting question sort

01:12:33.661 --> 01:12:34.890
of what is the threshold?

01:12:34.891 --> 01:12:36.295
Like how much error can we tolerate

01:12:36.296 --> 01:12:38.475
before qualitatively different behavior start happening?

01:12:38.476 --> 01:12:41.040
Yeah and a lot of that is largely everybody.

01:12:41.900 --> 01:12:46.495
&gt;&gt; Would it be practical in some cases to measure

01:12:46.496 --> 01:12:48.955
Beta from a group and then

01:12:48.956 --> 01:12:50.490
adjust your selection algorithms

01:12:50.491 --> 01:12:51.945
instead of trying to fix the bias?

01:12:51.946 --> 01:12:53.705
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's a nice question.

01:12:53.706 --> 01:12:55.940
Can we measure Beta and then maybe use theta?

01:12:55.941 --> 01:12:57.360
So there's two questions there.

01:12:57.361 --> 01:12:59.030
One, is it plausible that we might be

01:12:59.031 --> 01:13:01.350
able to measure some notion of Beta?

01:13:01.351 --> 01:13:03.310
They are, potentially, and

01:13:03.311 --> 01:13:05.015
we've thought a little about this.

01:13:05.016 --> 01:13:09.175
Because to the extent that alpha and Delta are known,

01:13:09.176 --> 01:13:10.800
the one missing thing,

01:13:10.801 --> 01:13:12.385
and the selection rate is known,

01:13:12.386 --> 01:13:15.415
the one missing thing there is Beta.

01:13:15.416 --> 01:13:18.300
And in some sense, some of the behavioral work

01:13:18.301 --> 01:13:19.570
has just started to get into

01:13:19.571 --> 01:13:21.035
trying to estimate that quantity

01:13:21.036 --> 01:13:23.520
mainly through lab experiments,

01:13:23.521 --> 01:13:25.140
although we could do it with historical data.

01:13:25.141 --> 01:13:27.340
Now the question, could we use that as an intervention?

01:13:27.341 --> 01:13:29.030
We could say, what you should do

01:13:29.031 --> 01:13:31.320
is just adjust your estimates by Beta,

01:13:31.321 --> 01:13:33.660
that will correct for the bias.

01:13:33.661 --> 01:13:35.430
I think the challenge there

01:13:35.431 --> 01:13:37.950
is one very important thing about the Rooney Rule,

01:13:37.951 --> 01:13:40.890
and actually everything in the analysis that we did,

01:13:40.891 --> 01:13:43.550
is that it never requires people

01:13:43.551 --> 01:13:47.275
to write down a number and manipulate the number.

01:13:47.276 --> 01:13:48.770
All it ever does is ask people to make

01:13:48.771 --> 01:13:50.290
ordinal comparisons between things.

01:13:50.291 --> 01:13:53.190
And what that's consistent with is the fact that in

01:13:53.191 --> 01:13:55.430
the field work on this

01:13:55.431 --> 01:13:57.580
and also some of the lab work, it's very hard.

01:13:57.581 --> 01:13:59.560
You would like to say we really care

01:13:59.561 --> 01:14:01.180
about Google Scholar accounts

01:14:01.181 --> 01:14:02.480
just as a pedagogical feature,

01:14:02.481 --> 01:14:04.350
we don't necessarily really have that [inaudible] feature,

01:14:04.351 --> 01:14:07.010
it's very hard to get people to say,

01:14:07.011 --> 01:14:08.680
I'm going to look at a candidate and say,

01:14:08.681 --> 01:14:10.665
future Google Scholar account is 12,000.

01:14:10.666 --> 01:14:12.870
This one, future Google Scholar account, 14,000.

01:14:12.871 --> 01:14:14.160
Okay, that person wins, but I'm going to

01:14:14.161 --> 01:14:16.300
divide down by 1.2 because of bias.

01:14:16.301 --> 01:14:17.750
Even before you ever got to the factor 1.2,

01:14:17.751 --> 01:14:20.290
the idea that you're going to write down estimates,

01:14:20.291 --> 01:14:21.610
that seems very hard for people to do.

01:14:21.611 --> 01:14:23.140
And so what people are doing,

01:14:23.141 --> 01:14:24.970
this is a model,

01:14:24.971 --> 01:14:26.475
which is an abstraction barrier.

01:14:26.476 --> 01:14:27.830
There are numbers operating below

01:14:27.831 --> 01:14:29.060
the surface but all the people are ever

01:14:29.061 --> 01:14:30.300
doing is making ordinal comparisons

01:14:30.301 --> 01:14:31.510
and the intervention's operating there.

01:14:31.511 --> 01:14:34.750
But I think it's very interesting in mapping.

01:14:34.751 --> 01:14:37.590
&gt;&gt; Couldn't K be the intervention?

01:14:37.591 --> 01:14:37.880
&gt;&gt; Couldn't?

01:14:37.881 --> 01:14:40.440
&gt;&gt; K be intervention while you address the threshold?

01:14:40.441 --> 01:14:41.755
&gt;&gt; So, how big should the shortlist be?

01:14:41.756 --> 01:14:42.520
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:14:42.521 --> 01:14:43.985
&gt;&gt; So you could have an intervention that says,

01:14:43.986 --> 01:14:46.570
you must continue interviewing people until you get to,

01:14:46.571 --> 01:14:51.390
say, a candidate from group X.

01:14:51.391 --> 01:14:53.985
&gt;&gt; No, I meant fixed K,

01:14:53.986 --> 01:14:56.790
a bigger K with a probability distribution, [inaudible] the data.

01:14:56.791 --> 01:14:58.060
Every year you pick

01:14:58.061 --> 01:15:01.160
a different K and does it not give you information?

01:15:01.161 --> 01:15:05.650
&gt;&gt; Obviously, if there's variation in K. Again,

01:15:05.651 --> 01:15:08.190
that would be helpful in estimating Beta.

01:15:08.191 --> 01:15:09.400
Again, I'm not sure that would help with

01:15:09.401 --> 01:15:11.605
people trying to form their own numerical estimates.

01:15:11.606 --> 01:15:13.140
I do think it's somehow key in

01:15:13.141 --> 01:15:14.700
these models that the agents were

01:15:14.701 --> 01:15:16.525
being modeled to make ordinal comparisons,

01:15:16.526 --> 01:15:17.740
not numerical evaluations.

01:15:17.741 --> 01:15:20.040
But, yes, if there were natural variation

01:15:20.041 --> 01:15:23.370
in K. The Rooney Rule as

01:15:23.371 --> 01:15:25.365
introduced so far says one out of K.

01:15:25.366 --> 01:15:28.130
But there's presumably some actual parameter

01:15:28.131 --> 01:15:29.340
like L out of K,

01:15:29.341 --> 01:15:32.010
you can imagine an Lth order Rooney rule,

01:15:32.011 --> 01:15:33.070
it says you must have L of

01:15:33.071 --> 01:15:34.880
your K slots for your room reserved for a group.

01:15:34.881 --> 01:15:37.380
And it's certainly not clear, in fact,

01:15:37.381 --> 01:15:38.690
obviously it's not the case that one

01:15:38.691 --> 01:15:40.030
is always the optimal number.

01:15:40.031 --> 01:15:42.010
Our comparison here has been about one versus zero,

01:15:42.011 --> 01:15:44.190
but one versus two versus three is very

01:15:44.191 --> 01:15:47.375
special. I'll let you keep going.

01:15:47.376 --> 01:15:48.950
&gt;&gt; So, in real life, if I'm going

01:15:48.951 --> 01:15:50.335
to interview K people for a job,

01:15:50.336 --> 01:15:51.620
that I'm going to give one person

01:15:51.621 --> 01:15:53.240
a job and you alluded to it a little bit in

01:15:53.241 --> 01:15:57.170
your graph of number of coaches.

01:15:57.171 --> 01:15:58.850
And you said, "Oh, maybe recently we've

01:15:58.851 --> 01:16:02.045
just decided that basically

01:16:02.046 --> 01:16:03.240
the decision is made in advance

01:16:03.241 --> 01:16:04.460
and I go through the motions of

01:16:04.461 --> 01:16:05.920
interviewing K people which include

01:16:05.921 --> 01:16:08.170
at least one underrepresented person."

01:16:08.171 --> 01:16:10.840
Have you done any thinking

01:16:10.841 --> 01:16:13.390
about is there something more we learn?

01:16:13.391 --> 01:16:14.870
Presumably we do the interviewing

01:16:14.871 --> 01:16:16.410
in a reasonable world to

01:16:16.411 --> 01:16:18.210
learn something more about the candidates in

01:16:18.211 --> 01:16:20.100
order to make a better informed decision,

01:16:20.101 --> 01:16:23.620
have you done any analysis on hidden information and

01:16:23.621 --> 01:16:24.870
how that might affect

01:16:24.871 --> 01:16:27.210
this model of who then actually gets a job?

01:16:27.211 --> 01:16:29.510
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, I think that's an interesting question.

01:16:29.511 --> 01:16:32.320
As you can see in the discussion of the max at the end,

01:16:32.321 --> 01:16:33.730
the way we're modelling theory

01:16:33.731 --> 01:16:36.180
is there's a random variables of some distribution.

01:16:36.181 --> 01:16:37.220
The act of interviewing now

01:16:37.221 --> 01:16:38.640
materializes the value from it,

01:16:38.641 --> 01:16:42.230
which is a very extreme form of the [inaudible] my uncertainty,

01:16:42.231 --> 01:16:43.585
now I have a deterministic value.

01:16:43.586 --> 01:16:47.125
You can imagine things

01:16:47.126 --> 01:16:48.970
and some of this shows up in some of

01:16:48.971 --> 01:16:51.330
the theoretical economics work on

01:16:51.331 --> 01:16:52.810
discrimination in the labor market which

01:16:52.811 --> 01:16:53.770
still uses very different models

01:16:53.771 --> 01:16:54.635
than we're talking about.

01:16:54.636 --> 01:16:56.560
Where for example there's a temporary

01:16:56.561 --> 01:16:57.970
labor market and a permanent labor market,

01:16:57.971 --> 01:17:00.890
so some nice work that Willy Hu

01:17:00.891 --> 01:17:04.750
and Yiling Chen at Harvard just looked at recently,

01:17:04.751 --> 01:17:07.020
where the temporary labor market gives

01:17:07.021 --> 01:17:08.300
me better information about

01:17:08.301 --> 01:17:09.390
the candidate but still not perfect.

01:17:09.391 --> 01:17:10.640
And then that allows me to make

01:17:10.641 --> 01:17:12.705
decisions about the permanent labor market.

01:17:12.706 --> 01:17:14.745
That allows us to model of things such as,

01:17:14.746 --> 01:17:17.700
I have a three-month internship program

01:17:17.701 --> 01:17:21.415
in which I learn some information about the candidate.

01:17:21.416 --> 01:17:23.705
But it's not that I now know everything.

01:17:23.706 --> 01:17:25.490
And so, this is

01:17:25.491 --> 01:17:27.010
an interesting staging effect

01:17:27.011 --> 01:17:29.630
where I could invest different amounts of effort.

01:17:29.631 --> 01:17:32.030
I could interview someone for one day or two days,

01:17:32.031 --> 01:17:33.230
I could give them an internship for

01:17:33.231 --> 01:17:34.415
two months or three months.

01:17:34.416 --> 01:17:36.620
I could do a variety of things

01:17:36.621 --> 01:17:38.690
that are going to come at different costs

01:17:38.691 --> 01:17:40.690
and increase the amount of

01:17:40.691 --> 01:17:41.780
information I have about this person or

01:17:41.781 --> 01:17:43.175
reduce the uncertainty of this person.

01:17:43.176 --> 01:17:45.500
So, I think some of the work in

01:17:45.501 --> 01:17:49.075
that domain may also provide some insight into it [inaudible].

01:17:49.076 --> 01:17:55.140
&gt;&gt; So, at the end,

01:17:55.141 --> 01:17:57.690
you mentioned this idea of the max and

01:17:57.691 --> 01:17:59.070
bringing it back to where you were earlier

01:17:59.071 --> 01:18:00.690
in definitions of fairness,

01:18:00.691 --> 01:18:02.655
calibration is expected value.

01:18:02.656 --> 01:18:04.590
And you could transform that from,

01:18:04.591 --> 01:18:06.395
instead of being 1-0 recidivism,

01:18:06.396 --> 01:18:08.855
to actually estimation of harm

01:18:08.856 --> 01:18:10.400
and take a max over a group where you

01:18:10.401 --> 01:18:11.840
start matching based on features.

01:18:11.841 --> 01:18:12.380
&gt;&gt; That's true, yes.

01:18:12.381 --> 01:18:14.130
&gt;&gt; If we start thinking in this rank-based rule

01:18:14.131 --> 01:18:15.840
and defaulting on loans,

01:18:15.841 --> 01:18:17.170
it can be actually again

01:18:17.171 --> 01:18:19.045
an unbounded variable of the amount lost.

01:18:19.046 --> 01:18:20.960
How do we start thinking of fairness

01:18:20.961 --> 01:18:22.710
in this role of ranking based on that?

01:18:22.711 --> 01:18:24.800
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's an interesting question,

01:18:24.801 --> 01:18:28.380
where we're doing some ordinal ranking.

01:18:28.450 --> 01:18:32.510
Yes, we looked a little bit at this,

01:18:32.511 --> 01:18:33.770
because ranking is actually a nice

01:18:33.771 --> 01:18:36.350
intervention when I'm trying to say,

01:18:36.351 --> 01:18:39.490
take an existing policy based on a human estimate of

01:18:39.491 --> 01:18:43.670
risk and then using some algorithmic intervention.

01:18:43.671 --> 01:18:44.960
So, one thing we've looked at

01:18:44.961 --> 01:18:46.390
in the empirical work that we've been doing on

01:18:46.391 --> 01:18:51.005
judges and bail is to say,

01:18:51.006 --> 01:18:52.560
let's imagine a judge

01:18:52.561 --> 01:18:54.925
that releases some number of people.

01:18:54.926 --> 01:18:56.430
We'd like to use the risk tool

01:18:56.431 --> 01:18:57.980
to sort them by predictive risk

01:18:57.981 --> 01:18:59.960
and basically detain the ones

01:18:59.961 --> 01:19:00.965
who are viewed as the most risky.

01:19:00.966 --> 01:19:01.770
All right, and so you can

01:19:01.771 --> 01:19:03.340
imagine some interaction between

01:19:03.341 --> 01:19:04.550
the human decision maker and

01:19:04.551 --> 01:19:06.160
the algorithm based on ranking,

01:19:06.161 --> 01:19:08.380
where the human picks the set,

01:19:08.381 --> 01:19:10.050
the algorithm ranks them and knocks the

01:19:10.051 --> 01:19:13.390
most risky few out of it.

01:19:13.391 --> 01:19:14.730
So, in that domain, we might then think

01:19:14.731 --> 01:19:17.380
about the balance between different groups,

01:19:17.381 --> 01:19:20.390
whether there's bias going on to different groups.

01:19:20.391 --> 01:19:22.120
We might say, for example, of

01:19:22.121 --> 01:19:23.680
this set that you knock out,

01:19:23.681 --> 01:19:25.360
we need to keep some kind of approximate

01:19:25.361 --> 01:19:27.360
composition between the groups.

01:19:27.361 --> 01:19:28.460
It would be a kind

01:19:28.461 --> 01:19:30.490
of Rooney Rule-like activity applied to

01:19:30.491 --> 01:19:32.845
the algorithm's decision to knock people off

01:19:32.846 --> 01:19:35.450
the top end of the max ranking.

01:19:35.451 --> 01:19:36.580
But, I think there's a lot

01:19:36.581 --> 01:19:37.610
of things you can potentially do,

01:19:37.611 --> 01:19:38.930
because there's now this interaction

01:19:38.931 --> 01:19:42.120
of the human decision maker's selection,

01:19:42.121 --> 01:19:44.000
the algorithm's sorting, and some notion

01:19:44.001 --> 01:19:47.380
of fairness as implied by the competition.

01:19:47.381 --> 01:19:49.020
So, I think there are a lot of

01:19:49.021 --> 01:19:50.175
interesting questions there that

01:19:50.176 --> 01:19:52.280
one could potentially try modeling.

01:19:52.310 --> 01:19:54.920
&gt;&gt; The expectation of max is

01:19:54.921 --> 01:19:57.320
an interesting thing and it just reminds me.

01:19:57.321 --> 01:19:59.420
With SAT, you can take it multiple

01:19:59.421 --> 01:20:01.150
times and then you just report the max,

01:20:01.151 --> 01:20:03.530
which sounds like weird ahead of time.

01:20:03.531 --> 01:20:04.660
But given what you've found,

01:20:04.661 --> 01:20:06.740
it's actually maybe a more correct thing to do than

01:20:06.741 --> 01:20:08.430
just allow people to take it once

01:20:08.431 --> 01:20:10.430
to get just an expected average value.

01:20:10.431 --> 01:20:11.900
And what if they're similar,

01:20:11.901 --> 01:20:15.090
like when 10 people interview the candidate,

01:20:15.091 --> 01:20:17.540
perhaps we should do 10 really tough interviews

01:20:17.541 --> 01:20:19.950
and take the max response.

01:20:19.951 --> 01:20:21.180
If one person liked them,

01:20:21.181 --> 01:20:23.025
we keep them, instead of averaging.

01:20:23.026 --> 01:20:26.020
&gt;&gt; That actually is the mean.

01:20:28.040 --> 01:20:32.520
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] I think we're [inaudible]

01:20:32.521 --> 01:20:34.490
the average or the total or the sum or

01:20:34.491 --> 01:20:36.730
whatever that the max is a different world.

01:20:36.731 --> 01:20:38.480
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:20:38.481 --> 01:20:42.360
Now, I should say it's going to depend,

01:20:42.361 --> 01:20:43.750
this is in a world where you're optimizing

01:20:43.751 --> 01:20:45.360
for the max performance.

01:20:45.361 --> 01:20:48.030
So, I think if you were going

01:20:48.031 --> 01:20:51.570
to be hiring for a commune of

01:20:51.571 --> 01:20:52.840
deep thinkers who were going to sit in

01:20:52.841 --> 01:20:54.600
their rooms and have great idea and then out [inaudible]

01:20:54.601 --> 01:20:58.590
come the best idea of all of them, then yes.

01:20:58.591 --> 01:21:00.180
Qualitatively, if you have

01:21:00.181 --> 01:21:02.650
a candidate who really wows somebody in one interaction,

01:21:02.651 --> 01:21:05.225
you're like, that's what we're trying to select for.

01:21:05.226 --> 01:21:07.570
Because when I pick K people,

01:21:07.571 --> 01:21:08.710
and I just got to pick the max,

01:21:08.711 --> 01:21:11.155
I'm going to get the top whatever K quantile somebody,

01:21:11.156 --> 01:21:12.700
that's intuitively what's happening.

01:21:12.701 --> 01:21:15.130
On the other hand, if you're picking, say,

01:21:15.131 --> 01:21:16.350
for a research lab where

01:21:16.351 --> 01:21:17.710
everybody is producing output and

01:21:17.711 --> 01:21:19.310
we're evaluating something much more like

01:21:19.311 --> 01:21:21.585
an LP norm closer to L1 or L2,

01:21:21.586 --> 01:21:24.110
then I may not be optimizing for the max.

01:21:24.111 --> 01:21:26.655
&gt;&gt; But the candidate selection being based on a max like,

01:21:26.656 --> 01:21:28.150
say, Jerry's score or something

01:21:28.151 --> 01:21:30.010
is actually the more correct.

01:21:30.011 --> 01:21:31.950
&gt;&gt; That one I think, and you can

01:21:31.951 --> 01:21:34.110
even argue other aspects of college applications.

01:21:34.111 --> 01:21:37.850
So, candidates often try many different, say,

01:21:37.851 --> 01:21:40.365
extracurricular activities and they

01:21:40.366 --> 01:21:41.945
might try six or eight of them.

01:21:41.946 --> 01:21:43.380
If they really succeed at one, that's what

01:21:43.381 --> 01:21:44.690
they write about in their application essay.

01:21:44.691 --> 01:21:45.620
And so implicitly, the system is

01:21:45.621 --> 01:21:46.780
set up for them to select

01:21:46.781 --> 01:21:48.550
the max of all the activities

01:21:48.551 --> 01:21:50.580
that they try, maybe that makes sense.

01:21:50.581 --> 01:21:52.055
You need to think of this

01:21:52.056 --> 01:21:53.230
in completely different domains.

01:21:53.231 --> 01:21:54.960
If you're on vacation for five days in

01:21:54.961 --> 01:21:57.020
some new place and you're

01:21:57.021 --> 01:21:58.190
going to eat five dinners at

01:21:58.191 --> 01:22:00.930
five restaurants and you're hedonic experience,

01:22:00.931 --> 01:22:01.990
the whole thing is going to be the

01:22:01.991 --> 01:22:03.945
best of the five dinners that you had.

01:22:03.946 --> 01:22:04.840
It says you should actually

01:22:04.841 --> 01:22:06.325
evaluate Yelp reviews differently.

01:22:06.326 --> 01:22:08.650
You should be looking at upside because in the end,

01:22:08.651 --> 01:22:11.115
you remember the best of those five, not the median.

01:22:11.116 --> 01:22:13.620
&gt;&gt; That's the meaning also remember.

01:22:13.621 --> 01:22:16.440
&gt;&gt; The meaning also remember, yes.

01:22:16.441 --> 01:22:18.460
&gt;&gt; Just the last question before we wrap up.

01:22:18.461 --> 01:22:21.090
&gt;&gt; So, the first part,

01:22:21.091 --> 01:22:22.900
you talked about this group calibration

01:22:22.901 --> 01:22:24.390
as one of the alternatives.

01:22:24.391 --> 01:22:26.130
So if you were to drop that,

01:22:26.131 --> 01:22:28.075
what would you want to replace it with?

01:22:28.076 --> 01:22:29.970
&gt;&gt; Well, in a way,

01:22:29.971 --> 01:22:31.650
I want to keep the other two.

01:22:31.651 --> 01:22:32.930
So I had three things that were mutual.

01:22:32.931 --> 01:22:35.410
&gt;&gt; Keep those two and then drop calibration,

01:22:35.411 --> 01:22:37.380
what do you replaced it with?

01:22:37.381 --> 01:22:39.940
&gt;&gt; Well, I guess I should

01:22:39.941 --> 01:22:42.320
check to make sure I understand the question.

01:22:42.321 --> 01:22:44.620
But, I have three things right now,

01:22:44.621 --> 01:22:47.090
calibration, balance for positive, balance for negative.

01:22:47.091 --> 01:22:48.615
They're mutually incompatible

01:22:48.616 --> 01:22:49.740
and sometimes I have to drop one.

01:22:49.741 --> 01:22:51.800
So, one of the arguments

01:22:51.801 --> 01:22:54.090
in this work of hard price and [inaudible] makes this argument.

01:22:54.091 --> 01:22:56.695
If we drop calibration, then we're actually just right.

01:22:56.696 --> 01:22:58.520
We don't have put anything back in because we're now just

01:22:58.521 --> 01:23:00.350
barely going to satisfy that.

01:23:00.351 --> 01:23:01.550
So we're gonna equalize the positives,

01:23:01.551 --> 01:23:02.950
equalize negatives and we now have

01:23:02.951 --> 01:23:04.865
lost all our degrees of freedom to do anything else.

01:23:04.866 --> 01:23:05.705
And now we're just going to try

01:23:05.706 --> 01:23:07.285
optimizing accuracy with it.

01:23:07.286 --> 01:23:09.330
If I really were to put third condition back in,

01:23:09.331 --> 01:23:11.010
I might get to some new impossibility result,

01:23:11.011 --> 01:23:12.250
which I couldn't handle.

01:23:12.251 --> 01:23:16.990
So, the argument that they make in their work is,

01:23:16.991 --> 01:23:18.510
let's think about situations where

01:23:18.511 --> 01:23:19.850
a calibration is not the important thing.

01:23:19.851 --> 01:23:22.660
What's important is that people who have

01:23:22.661 --> 01:23:24.070
similar behaviors should be given

01:23:24.071 --> 01:23:26.240
similar scores and that that's really what fairness is,

